/**
 * Concept micro-notes: three short blocks for the concepts that have no content
 * of their own.
 *
 * 847 of the 1,089 concepts have nothing attached, so their page shows the
 * *chapter's* notes - written for the chapter as a whole, which rarely answers
 * "what is this concept?". A micro-note answers exactly that in three lines:
 * what it is, why it works, and the mistake students actually make.
 *
 * Keyed by `<chapter code>|<concept name>` because concept names repeat across
 * chapters. Applied to a database by `ContentService.syncConceptNotes` (and by
 * the seed on a fresh install); the note becomes a NOTES content item on that
 * concept.
 */
export const CONCEPT_MICRO_NOTES: Record<string, string> = {
  'ENG-10-1|Humble Faith and Hope':
    '**What it is:** Lencho\'s unshakable belief that God will answer his letter, held by a poor farmer with nothing else to fall back on. **Why it works:** Because he trusts completely, the ruined harvest never becomes despair - he asks God for a hundred pesos and waits, and the postmaster keeps that trust alive by sending seventy signed \'God\'. **Common mistake:** Reading his faith as silliness instead of as the strength that keeps him going.',
  'ENG-10-1|Irony in the Story':
    '**What it is:** The gap between what Lencho believes and what you know, snapped shut in the story\'s last lines. **Why it works:** The post-office staff give their own money out of kindness, and Lencho, counting seventy of the hundred pesos, calls them \'a bunch of crooks\' - so you laugh while he grieves. **Common mistake:** Calling any bad luck irony; here it needs the contrast between the helpers and the accusation.',
  'ENG-10-1|The Letter to God':
    '**What it is:** The single act that drives the story - a farmer writing to God for money after hail flattens his crop. **Why it works:** Lencho asks for a hundred pesos to sow again and buy food; the postmaster collects from his staff and friends, only seventy can be raised, and that shortfall is what turns the tale. **Common mistake:** Thinking the postmaster simply forwards the letter to God rather than quietly gathering money himself.',
  'ENG-10-2|Rhetoric of Freedom and Equality':
    '**What it is:** Mandela\'s way of arguing for a non-racial South Africa through images and balanced contrasts instead of plain statements. **Why it works:** He sets two anthems side by side, calls the oppressor as much a prisoner as the oppressed, and says courage is \'not the absence of fear but the triumph over it\' - paradoxes that make you feel the idea. **Common mistake:** Treating these lines as decoration rather than as the argument itself.',
  'ENG-10-2|The Inauguration of Freedom':
    '**What it is:** The ceremony of 10 May 1994 in the Union Buildings amphitheatre at Pretoria, where South Africa\'s first black president took office. **Why it works:** Two national anthems were sung - \'Die Stem\' and \'Nkosi Sikelel iAfrika\' - before the world\'s leaders, so the ceremony staged the reconciliation the speech asked for. **Common mistake:** Placing it in Johannesburg or Cape Town, or forgetting that both anthems were sung.',
  'ENG-10-2|Values of Sacrifice and Leadership':
    '**What it is:** Mandela\'s belief that freedom was bought by others\' sacrifice and can only be kept by courage, not revenge. **Why it works:** He thanks the heroes who died for it and argues the oppressor is a prisoner of hatred, so freeing the oppressed frees the oppressor; leadership is standing up again after fear, not being fearless. **Common mistake:** Saying he wanted oppressors to stay oppressed, when he wants both sides freed.',
  'ENG-10-3|Courage and Overcoming Fear':
    '**What it is:** The thread joining the two flying stories - the young seagull and the pilot each beat fear by acting anyway. **Why it works:** The seagull flies because hunger outweighs his fear of the ledge, and the Dakota pilot enters the storm because he wants to reach home, so a strong purpose tips the balance before confidence arrives. **Common mistake:** Thinking courage means feeling no fear at all instead of acting while afraid.',
  'ENG-10-3|Descriptive Writing and Imagery':
    '**What it is:** Language that lets you see and hear a scene, the strongest tool in both flying stories. **Why it works:** Simile and physical detail do the work: the storm clouds are \'black mountains standing in front of him\', the sky is a blanket of dark clouds, and inside the cloud everything turns black while the instruments die. **Common mistake:** Thinking any adjective counts as imagery; a picture needs comparison or exact physical detail.',
  'ENG-10-3|The Flying Machine':
    '**What it is:** The old Dakota, a small single-engine plane the narrator flies himself towards England. **Why it works:** When the compass and radio fail inside the storm he follows an unknown black aeroplane, which guides him down to the runway and then vanishes, so the machine that saved him is never explained and the story keeps its mystery. **Common mistake:** Assuming the airport sent a rescue plane; nobody there had seen one.',
  'ENG-10-4|Diary Entry Style':
    '**What it is:** Writing addressed to a private listener, which is how Anne\'s diary works from its first page. **Why it works:** She names the diary \'Kitty\' and opens each entry as a letter to a friend, so confession comes naturally - she can admit to paper what she would never say aloud, and the dates keep the record honest. **Common mistake:** Treating it as a formal log of events; it is a conversation full of feelings and opinions.',
  'ENG-10-4|Hope and Humanity in Adversity':
    '**What it is:** Anne\'s stubborn belief, held while in hiding, that people are good at heart. **Why it works:** Even in wartime she records ordinary life - the class quaking over promotions, Mr Keesing\'s essays on \'A Chatterbox\', her comic poem that makes everyone laugh - and that humour keeps her faith in people alive. **Common mistake:** Reading her as cheerful and untouched by fear; the hope is a choice she keeps making under threat.',
  'ENG-10-4|Life in Hiding':
    '**What it is:** The cramped, silent existence Anne and her family led in the secret annexe during the German occupation. **Why it works:** Cut off from friends her own age, she turns to paper - \'paper has more patience than people\' - so the diary is not a hobby but the one place she can speak freely about the fear and the boredom. **Common mistake:** Forgetting she had no close friend to confide in, which is exactly why Kitty exists.',
  'ENG-10-5|Portuguese Heritage in Goa':
    '**What it is:** The mark centuries of Portuguese rule left on ordinary Goan life, most visible in its bread. **Why it works:** The Portuguese brought the baker\'s trade, so the pao and the sweet bolinhas baked for Christmas and every festival are living evidence rather than museum pieces, and the baker\'s rounds shaped childhood memory. **Common mistake:** Looking only for old churches and forts; the chapter points you to food and daily work.',
  'ENG-10-5|The Bangle Sellers':
    '**What it is:** Sarojini Naidu\'s poem in which the sellers\' cry turns bangles into a picture of a woman\'s life. **Why it works:** Each colour is matched to a stage - bridal red and gold for the hopeful bride, purple and grey for the mature matron - so a market stall becomes a picture of growing up, and the bangles echo the Indian landscape. **Common mistake:** Reading it only as a shop scene; the bangles are being compared to a life.',
  'ENG-10-5|Words of Trade and Daily Life':
    '**What it is:** The working vocabulary of the Goa section - kabai, pao, bolinhas - which carries a place\'s history inside ordinary objects. **Why it works:** The kabai was the baker\'s long one-piece frock down to his knees and the pao was the bread he sold, so one word fixes a trade, a costume and an era; his plump body proved baking fed a family. **Common mistake:** Confusing the kabai with the bread or the basket instead of the baker\'s dress.',
  'ENG-10-6|First Person Narrative':
    '**What it is:** Telling the story as \'I\' - Maxwell\'s own voice recounting his life with Mij. **Why it works:** Because he reports what he saw and felt, small details such as Mij turning the chromium taps or playing with marbles read as memory rather than report, so his worry on the flight feels immediate. **Common mistake:** Thinking first person guarantees truth; it guarantees intimacy and bias, since you only know what the narrator noticed.',
  'ENG-10-6|The Return of the Otter':
    '**What it is:** The last stretch of Mij\'s story - the otter settled into Maxwell\'s London flat and finally handed over to the zoo. **Why it works:** Water is his element, so the bathroom becomes his playground and he splashes it everywhere; the bond was built at home, and the ending admits that a wild animal cannot stay a pet. **Common mistake:** Believing Mij was simply set free in the wild at the end of the chapter.',
  'ENG-10-6|Vocabulary of Animals and Nature':
    '**What it is:** The animal-and-outdoors word stock of the otter chapter - somersault, splash, claw, species. **Why it works:** Knowing a somersault is a complete roll of the body lets you see Mij tumbling in water, and knowing zoologists named him Lutrogale perspicillata maxwelli tells you he was a kind of otter no one had described before. **Common mistake:** Reading \'somersault\' as a jump forward instead of a full turn over.',
  'ENG-10-7|A Moment of Life and Death':
    '**What it is:** The dead cow by the road that turns Valli\'s joyful bus ride into her first brush with death. **Why it works:** On the way out the same cow was young and alive; on the way back it lies dead, and that repetition makes the change strike her, so her excitement drains because she suddenly sees how fragile life is. **Common mistake:** Thinking something frightening spoiled the trip; nothing threatened her, she simply understood loss.',
  'ENG-10-7|Descriptive Language':
    '**What it is:** The exact seeing that fills Valli\'s ride - canal, palm trees, grassland, mountains, blue sky, green fields. **Why it works:** The writing stays at a child\'s eye level and names concrete things in the order the bus passes them, while small touches like the conductor calling her \'Madam\' tell you how the world treats her. **Common mistake:** Believing long words make description better; precise ordinary detail does the work.',
  'ENG-10-7|Words of Travel and Places':
    '**What it is:** The vocabulary of a bus journey - fare, conductor, town, journey - the words Valli needs for her trip. **Why it works:** Numbers anchor the words: the ride to town takes forty-five minutes and the fare is thirty paise each way, so \'journey\' stops being abstract and becomes a treat she saves sixty paise for over weeks. **Common mistake:** Mixing up how long the ride took with how long she saved.',
  'ENG-10-8|Sermon as a Form of Speech':
    '**What it is:** A talk on a moral or religious subject, meant to teach and to console rather than to win an argument. **Why it works:** The Buddha does not lecture Kisa Gotami with the fact of death; he sends her from house to house for mustard seeds, so she discovers the truth herself and the lesson comforts instead of merely informing. **Common mistake:** Treating a sermon as a debate; its aim is to change how the listener feels and lives.',
  'ENG-10-8|The Sermon at Benares':
    '**What it is:** The Buddha\'s first teaching, given after Kisa Gotami begs him to bring her dead son back. **Why it works:** He asks for a handful of mustard seeds from a house where no one has died; she finds none, because every house has lost someone, and so learns that death is common to all and grief cannot be escaped by grieving. **Common mistake:** Thinking the seeds had power of their own; the errand was a lesson, not a medicine.',
  'ENG-10-8|Vocabulary from the Text':
    '**What it is:** The key words that carry the sermon - sermon, grief, mustard seed, the living and the dead. **Why it works:** \'Sermon\' means a religious or moral discourse, so when villagers say \'the living are few, but the dead are many\' you hear a claim about every household, which explains why the search for seeds must fail. **Common mistake:** Taking that line as a literal count of people instead of a statement that loss is universal.',
  'ENG-10-9|A Russian Wedding':
    '**What it is:** The setting and social world of the play - rural Russia, where a marriage is arranged between neighbouring landowners. **Why it works:** Lomov, thirty-five and anxious, comes to ask for Natalya, twenty-five; the match matters because it joins two estates, which is why Chubukov calls him \'my darling\' the moment he arrives. **Common mistake:** Reading the play as a love story; property, not romance, brings them together.',
  'ENG-10-9|Formal Letter Writing':
    '**What it is:** The fixed, polite shape of a letter written to someone you do not know personally. **Why it works:** Open with \'Dear Sir or Madam\', state the request in one plain sentence, close with \'Yours faithfully\' and your full name - as Lomov does when he asks for Natalya\'s hand and calls it an honour. **Common mistake:** Ending a letter that began \'Dear Sir\' with \'Yours sincerely\' or with your first name.',
  'ENG-10-9|Humour and Verbal Comedy':
    '**What it is:** Comedy built out of talk - the way these characters insult each other while trying to stay polite. **Why it works:** The joke is the switch. Lomov and Natalya forget the proposal and fight over Oxen Meadows and their dogs, then turn tender the instant marriage is mentioned again, and the rapid reversals make you laugh at them. **Common mistake:** Treating the quarrel as serious plot; it is the engine and ends in agreement.',
  'MAT-10-1|LCM':
    '**What it is:** The smallest number that two or more given numbers divide exactly. **Why it works:** Take the highest power of every prime from the factorisations: 6 = 2 x 3 and 20 = 2^2 x 5, so LCM = 2^2 x 3 x 5 = 60. Bells ringing every 18 and 24 minutes next ring together after LCM(18,24) = 72 minutes. **Common mistake:** Multiplying the numbers when they share factors - 18 x 24 is not the answer.',
  'MAT-10-1|Proving Irrationality':
    '**What it is:** Showing a number cannot be written as a fraction p/q of integers, usually by contradiction. **Why it works:** Assume root 2 = p/q in lowest terms; squaring gives p^2 = 2q^2, so 2 divides p^2 and hence p, then q too - contradicting \'lowest terms\'. The key fact is that if a prime p divides a^2, then p divides a. **Common mistake:** Stopping at \'p is even\' without reaching the contradiction.',
  'MAT-10-10|Cyclic Quadrilateral':
    '**What it is:** A quadrilateral whose four vertices all lie on one circle. **Why it works:** Opposite angles are supplementary, adding to 180 degrees, because each pair subtends the two arcs that together make the whole circle; so if angle A = 70 then angle C = 110. **Common mistake:** Applying \'opposite angles are equal\', which is true for a parallelogram, not for this shape.',
  'MAT-10-10|Length of Tangent':
    '**What it is:** The straight distance from a point outside a circle to the point where the tangent touches it. **Why it works:** The radius at the point of contact is perpendicular to the tangent, making a right triangle: tangent = root(OP^2 - r^2). With OP = 13 and r = 5 that is root(169 - 25) = 12 cm, and the two tangents from one point are equal. **Common mistake:** Doing OP - r = 13 - 5 = 8 instead of using Pythagoras.',
  'MAT-10-10|Perpendicular from Centre':
    '**What it is:** The line dropped from a circle\'s centre at right angles to a chord. **Why it works:** It always bisects the chord, so half the chord, the distance and the radius form a right triangle: (chord/2)^2 + d^2 = r^2. A 16 cm chord in a radius 10 circle has half 8, so d = root(100 - 64) = 6 cm. **Common mistake:** Putting the whole 16 cm chord into Pythagoras and halving nothing.',
  'MAT-10-11|Composite Figures':
    '**What it is:** Shapes made by joining or cutting familiar figures - squares, circles, semicircles, rings. **Why it works:** Split the shape into parts you know, work out each area, then add or subtract. A circle inscribed in a 14 cm square leaves 196 - 154 = 42 cm^2, since its radius is 7 and its area is 154 with pi = 22/7. **Common mistake:** Using the square\'s side as the circle\'s radius instead of half of it.',
  'MAT-10-11|Minor and Major Segment':
    '**What it is:** The two pieces a chord cuts a circle into - the smaller slice and the rest of the disc. **Why it works:** A segment is the sector minus the triangle formed by the two radii and the chord. For a 90 degree chord in radius 7 with pi = 22/7, the sector is 38.5 and the triangle 24.5, so the minor segment is 14 cm^2. **Common mistake:** Forgetting to subtract the triangle and quoting the sector area as the segment.',
  'MAT-10-11|Sector Area':
    '**What it is:** The pie-slice region between two radii and the arc they cut off. **Why it works:** It is a fraction of the whole circle: area = (theta/360) x pi x r^2. For theta = 90 and r = 14 with pi = 22/7 you get a quarter of 616, which is 154 cm^2, the same as a quadrant. **Common mistake:** Using the diameter in place of r, or forgetting the theta/360 fraction.',
  'MAT-10-12|Combination of Solids':
    '**What it is:** One object built by joining basic solids, such as a cone on a cylinder or a capsule. **Why it works:** Add the curved surfaces and count a flat base only where it is actually exposed, because the face where two solids meet is hidden inside. A cone mounted on a hemisphere needs only the cone\'s curved area plus the hemisphere\'s. **Common mistake:** Counting the shared circular face, which double-counts it and inflates the answer.',
  'MAT-10-12|Frustum':
    '**What it is:** The lower piece left when a cone is sliced parallel to its base, so it has two different radii. **Why it works:** Volume = (1/3) pi h (r1^2 + r1 r2 + r2^2) and slant height l = root(h^2 + (r1 - r2)^2), so curved area = pi (r1 + r2) l. With r1 = 5, r2 = 3, l = 4 it is pi x 8 x 4 = 32 pi cm^2. **Common mistake:** Using the full cone formula with one radius, or taking l equal to h.',
  'MAT-10-12|Surface Area Change':
    '**What it is:** How cutting or joining solids changes total surface area even though the volume stays the same. **Why it works:** Every new cut exposes fresh faces, so area grows; joining hides them, so area shrinks. A 6 cm cube cut into 2 cm cubes gives 27 cubes x 24 = 648 cm^2 against 216 before. **Common mistake:** Assuming area is conserved, or forgetting a halved sphere adds two flat circles, 2 pi r^2.',
  'MAT-10-13|Grouped Data':
    '**What it is:** Data given as class intervals with frequencies rather than as individual values. **Why it works:** Use the class mark, the midpoint, as each group\'s representative value and take a weighted mean, sum(f x x) / sum(f). For 10-20: 5, 20-30: 8, 30-40: 7 the marks are 15, 25, 35, so the mean is (75 + 200 + 245)/20 = 26. **Common mistake:** Averaging the class limits instead of the midpoints.',
  'MAT-10-13|Less Than and More Than':
    '**What it is:** Cumulative frequency tables that keep a running total up to, or above, each class boundary. **Why it works:** \'Less than\' plots upper limits against the running total and \'more than\' plots lower limits against what remains. If cumulative counts are 6, 15, 25, the middle class holds 15 - 6 = 9 observations. **Common mistake:** Plotting the midpoint instead of the boundary, or reading a running total as one class\'s frequency.',
  'MAT-10-13|Median from Ogive':
    '**What it is:** Reading the middle value of grouped data off a cumulative frequency curve. **Why it works:** Draw the less-than ogive, mark n/2 on the vertical axis, go across to the curve and down to the horizontal axis; with total frequency 60 you read at 30. Where the two ogives cross, the x-coordinate is the median, so a crossing at (35, 20) means 35. **Common mistake:** Reading the median off the frequency axis instead of the value axis.',
  'MAT-10-14|Compound Events':
    '**What it is:** One event made of two or more things happening, such as \'at least one tail\' or \'a sum of 7\'. **Why it works:** List the favourable outcomes in the sample space and divide. Two coins give 4 outcomes and 3 have a tail, so 3/4; two dice give 36 outcomes and 6 give a sum of 7, so 1/6. **Common mistake:** Forgetting \'at least one\' includes two, or treating draws without replacement as independent.',
  'MAT-10-14|Events':
    '**What it is:** A set of outcomes from an experiment - a single result, or several grouped as one. **Why it works:** P(E) = favourable outcomes / total outcomes, so with 17 balls (5 red, 8 white, 4 green) not-green is 13/17, and P(not E) = 1 - P(E): if P(E) = 0.35 then P(not E) = 0.65. **Common mistake:** Answering the complement when the question asked for the event itself.',
  'MAT-10-14|Sample Space':
    '**What it is:** The complete list of every possible outcome of an experiment. **Why it works:** Multiply the choices at each step: one coin twice gives 2 x 2 = 4 outcomes, three tosses give 2^3 = 8, and two dice give 6 x 6 = 36. Write the list out (HH, HT, TH, TT) before you count anything. **Common mistake:** Writing HH, HT, TT and losing TH - order matters, so both mixed results count.',
  'MAT-10-15|Construction':
    '**What it is:** Drawing an exact figure with ruler and compass only, each step justified by a theorem. **Why it works:** The radius at the point of contact is perpendicular to the tangent, so you erect a perpendicular there; for tangents from an outside point the circle on OP as diameter cuts the circle where they touch, and 60 degrees between tangents needs 120 at the centre. **Common mistake:** Drawing freehand or skipping the perpendicular.',
  'MAT-10-15|Golden Ratio':
    '**What it is:** The division of a segment so the whole is to the longer part as the longer part is to the shorter. **Why it works:** With whole = 1 and longer part x, 1/x = x/(1 - x), giving x^2 + x - 1 = 0 and x = (root 5 - 1)/2, about 0.618; the ratio of whole to part is about 1.618 and shows up in art and nature. **Common mistake:** Quoting 1.618 as the longer part, when that part of a unit segment is 0.618.',
  'MAT-10-15|Tangent from External Point':
    '**What it is:** The two straight lines you can draw from a point outside a circle that just touch it. **Why it works:** Each is perpendicular to a radius, so tangent = root(OP^2 - r^2), which is root(169 - 25) = 12 cm when OP = 13. The two tangents are equal, and the angle at the centre is 180 minus the angle between them, so 70 gives 110. **Common mistake:** Drawing only one tangent, or adding r to OP instead of using the right triangle.',
  'MAT-10-2|Remainder Theorem':
    '**What it is:** The shortcut that finds the remainder of a polynomial division without doing the long division. **Why it works:** Dividing p(x) by (x - a) leaves p(a). For x^3 - 3x^2 + 2x - 1 at x = 2: 8 - 12 + 4 - 1 = -1, so the remainder is -1; for x^3 - 4x^2 + 5x - 2 at 2 you get 0, so x - 2 is a factor. **Common mistake:** Flipping the sign and substituting x = -2 when the divisor is x - 2.',
  'MAT-10-3|Coincident Lines':
    '**What it is:** Two equations that describe exactly the same straight line, so every point of one lies on the other. **Why it works:** All three coefficients are in one proportion: a1/a2 = b1/b2 = c1/c2. 4x + 6y = 12 and kx + 9y = 18 coincide when 4/k = 6/9 = 12/18, giving k = 6, and the pair then has infinitely many solutions. **Common mistake:** Comparing only the x and y coefficients and ignoring the constant term.',
  'MAT-10-3|Cross Multiplication':
    '**What it is:** A formula-based way to solve two linear equations at once, with no elimination step. **Why it works:** Write both as a1x + b1y + c1 = 0, then x = (b1c2 - b2c1)/(a1b2 - a2b1) and y = (c1a2 - c2a1)/(a1b2 - a2b1). If the denominator is 0 the lines are parallel or coincident, as with x + 2y = 5 and 2x + 4y = 10. **Common mistake:** Reversing the order of subtraction, which flips the sign of both answers.',
  'MAT-10-3|Substitution and Elimination':
    '**What it is:** Two reliable methods for solving a pair of equations in x and y. **Why it works:** Substitution rewrites one equation as x = ... and puts it into the other; elimination adds or subtracts so a variable cancels. Add x + y = 5 and x - y = 1 to get 2x = 6, so x = 3 and y = 2. **Common mistake:** Subtracting when the coefficients have opposite signs - for y and -y you add to cancel.',
  'MAT-10-4|Discriminant':
    '**What it is:** The value b^2 - 4ac, which tells you what kind of roots a quadratic has before you solve it. **Why it works:** It is the part under the square root in the quadratic formula. For 3x^2 - 2x + 5 it is 4 - 60 = -56, so there are no real roots; for x^2 - 4x + 4 it is 0, giving the equal roots x = 2. **Common mistake:** Squaring b without keeping its sign, or dropping the signs of a and c.',
  'MAT-10-4|Real and Complex Roots':
    '**What it is:** What the discriminant says about where a quadratic\'s roots lie. **Why it works:** Positive gives two distinct real roots, zero gives equal real roots, and negative gives none, because the parabola never meets the x-axis. So discriminant -9 means no real roots, and discriminant 0 means real and equal roots. **Common mistake:** Calling a negative discriminant \'no solution\' - the equation still has roots, they are just not real.',
  'MAT-10-5|Applications':
    '**What it is:** Spotting that a real situation - rungs, savings, production - grows or shrinks by a fixed amount each step. **Why it works:** Name a, d and n from the story, then use a_n = a + (n - 1)d. Rungs falling from 45 cm to 25 cm by 2 cm give n = 11; production of 600 in year 3 and 700 in year 7 gives d = 25 and a = 550. **Common mistake:** Counting steps as n instead of n - 1 between the first and last terms.',
  'MAT-10-5|Formula':
    '**What it is:** The closed expressions that give any term of an AP and the total of its first n terms. **Why it works:** a_n = a + (n - 1)d and S_n = (n/2)(a + l). For 3, 7, 11, ... the 20th term is 3 + 19 x 4 = 79, so S = 10 x (3 + 79) = 820; the first 20 natural numbers give 210. **Common mistake:** Using n where n - 1 belongs, or finding the last term and forgetting to add the terms up.',
  'MAT-10-6|Applications':
    '**What it is:** Using similar triangles to measure heights and distances you cannot reach - poles, towers, shadows. **Why it works:** Corresponding sides of similar triangles are proportional. A 6 m pole with a 4 m shadow and a tower with a 28 m shadow give h/28 = 6/4, so the tower is 42 m, and areas follow the square of that ratio. **Common mistake:** Setting areas in direct proportion to the sides instead of to their squares.',
  'MAT-10-6|Ratio of Areas':
    '**What it is:** How the areas of two similar triangles compare once you know their sides. **Why it works:** The area ratio is the square of any corresponding length ratio, so sides 3 : 5 give areas 9 : 25, while areas 49 : 64 give a side or median ratio of 7 : 8 because medians scale with the sides. **Common mistake:** Answering 3 : 5 for the areas, or forgetting to take the square root when you start from areas.',
  'MAT-10-6|Thales Theorem':
    '**What it is:** The basic proportionality theorem: a line parallel to one side of a triangle divides the other two sides in the same ratio. **Why it works:** With DE parallel to BC, AD/DB = AE/EC. Given AD = 3, DB = 5 and AE = 4.5, you get EC = 4.5 x 5/3 = 7.5 cm. **Common mistake:** Writing AD/AB = AE/EC and pairing the wrong segments, or turning the ratio upside down.',
  'MAT-10-7|Collinearity':
    '**What it is:** Three or more points lying on one straight line. **Why it works:** The triangle they form collapses to zero area, so put the three points into the area formula and set it to 0; (1, 2), (3, 6) and (5, 10) all satisfy y = 2x. Equally, the slopes between consecutive pairs must be identical. **Common mistake:** Relying on the distance formula alone - even gaps do not prove the points are on one line.',
  'MAT-10-7|Section Formula':
    '**What it is:** The coordinates of the point that cuts a segment in a given ratio. **Why it works:** P = ((m x2 + n x1)/(m + n), (m y2 + n y1)/(m + n)), and the midpoint is just the case m = n. Joining (-1, 7) and (4, -3) in the ratio 2 : 3 gives ((8 - 3)/5, (-6 + 21)/5) = (1, 3). **Common mistake:** Swapping m and n, which mirrors the point to the wrong side of the segment.',
  'MAT-10-7|Using Coordinates':
    '**What it is:** Turning a shape on the plane into numbers - area, midpoint, length - so geometry becomes arithmetic. **Why it works:** Area of a triangle = (1/2)|x1(y2 - y3) + x2(y3 - y1) + x3(y1 - y2)|. For (0, 0), (4, 0) and (0, 3) it gives 6 square units, matching half base times height. **Common mistake:** Dropping the modulus and reporting a negative area, or mixing x and y inside a bracket.',
  'MAT-10-8|Right Triangle':
    '**What it is:** The triangle with one 90 degree angle on which every trigonometric ratio is built. **Why it works:** With hypotenuse 13 and one leg 5, the other leg is root(169 - 25) = 12; sin is opposite/hypotenuse, cos adjacent/hypotenuse, tan opposite/adjacent. If tan = 3/4 the sides are 3, 4, 5, so sin = 3/5 and cos = 4/5. **Common mistake:** Taking the hypotenuse as the side next to the right angle instead of opposite it.',
  'MAT-10-8|Values of Standard Angles':
    '**What it is:** The fixed sine, cosine and tangent values for 0, 30, 45, 60 and 90 degrees. **Why it works:** They come from two triangles - the half equilateral with sides 1, root 3, 2 and the isosceles right with 1, 1, root 2 - so sin 30 = 1/2, cos 60 = 1/2, tan 45 = 1 and sin 90 = 1. **Common mistake:** Swapping sine and cosine for 30 and 60, or writing tan 45 as 0.',
  'MAT-10-9|Angle of Depression':
    '**What it is:** The angle measured downward from the horizontal to an object below the observer. **Why it works:** The line of sight starts level and dips, so the angle at the observer is the depression and tan of it = height / horizontal distance. From a 75 m lighthouse at 30 degrees the ship is 75 root 3 m away. **Common mistake:** Measuring from the vertical, or using an elevation angle when the object is lower.',
  'MAT-10-9|Surveying':
    '**What it is:** Measuring land and heights by turning real distances into right triangles. **Why it works:** An instrument such as the theodolite gives the angle, a tape gives the distance, and tan links them to the height. A 30 m tower seen at 30 degrees is 30 root 3 m away, and an eye at 45 degrees adds the observer\'s 1.5 m to reach 30 m. **Common mistake:** Forgetting to add the observer\'s height when the angle was measured from the eyes.',
  'MAT-10-9|Word Problems':
    '**What it is:** Turning a described situation - ladder, shadow, tower - into a triangle you can actually solve. **Why it works:** Sketch it, mark the right angle, label the known side and angle, then pick the ratio that uses both. A 10 m ladder reaching a window 8 m up has its foot root(100 - 64) = 6 m from the wall. **Common mistake:** Using tan when the ladder itself, the hypotenuse, is the known side.',
  'SCI-10-1|Displacement Reactions':
    '**What it is:** A reaction in which a more reactive element pushes a less reactive one out of its compound. **Why it works:** Reactivity decides the winner, so zinc, above copper, turns blue copper sulphate into colourless zinc sulphate and deposits copper: Zn + CuSO4 -> ZnSO4 + Cu. Iron does the same, coating the nail brown. **Common mistake:** Expecting copper to displace zinc, or calling any reaction that forms a metal a displacement.',
  'SCI-10-1|Redox Reactions':
    '**What it is:** A reaction in which one substance gains oxygen, or loses hydrogen, while another loses oxygen at the same time. **Why it works:** The two changes are tied together: in ZnO + C -> Zn + CO carbon is oxidised and zinc oxide is reduced, and in CuO + H2 -> Cu + H2O the hydrogen is the one oxidised. **Common mistake:** Labelling the substance that loses oxygen as oxidised - reduction is the removal of oxygen.',
  'SCI-10-10|Defects of Vision':
    '**What it is:** When the eye cannot focus a clear image on the retina - short sight, long sight, or the ageing eye. **Why it works:** Myopia focuses light in front of the retina, so a concave diverging lens spreads the rays and pushes the image back; hypermetropia needs a convex lens, and normal near vision is 25 cm. **Common mistake:** Prescribing a convex lens for myopia, or mixing up which defect needs which lens.',
  'SCI-10-10|Prism and Spectrum':
    '**What it is:** The fan of colours a glass prism produces from white light. **Why it works:** Each colour slows by a different amount in glass, so each bends differently - that is dispersion - and violet bends most while red bends least, giving VIBGYOR on the screen. The band itself is the spectrum. **Common mistake:** Saying the prism adds colour, or naming red as the most deviated.',
  'SCI-10-10|Rainbow':
    '**What it is:** A natural spectrum in the sky, made when sunlight passes through water droplets in the air. **Why it works:** Light refracts and disperses entering a droplet, reflects off the inside back surface, then refracts again as it leaves, so the colours spread out with red on the outside of the arc and violet inside. **Common mistake:** Stopping at refraction and leaving out the internal reflection that sends the light back to you.',
  'SCI-10-11|Energy Consumption':
    '**What it is:** The electricity a device uses, measured in the unit your bill is charged in. **Why it works:** Energy = power x time, and one kilowatt hour is 1000 W for 3600 s, that is 3.6 x 10^6 J. A 100 W bulb burning for 10 hours uses 0.1 x 10 = 1 kW h. **Common mistake:** Keeping power in watts and time in seconds, then calling that answer kilowatt hours.',
  'SCI-10-11|Resistivity':
    '**What it is:** How strongly a material itself opposes current, quite apart from the size of the piece. **Why it works:** R = rho L / A, so rho depends only on the material and its temperature - copper low, nichrome high - and its unit is the ohm metre, because length over area adds a metre. **Common mistake:** Thinking resistivity drops when you cut the wire shorter; the resistance falls, the material\'s resistivity does not.',
  'SCI-10-12|Faraday\'s Law':
    '**What it is:** The rule that a changing magnetic flux through a coil produces an emf, and so a current, in it. **Why it works:** Only a change counts - move a magnet in or out, or vary the nearby current - and the faster the flux changes the larger the induced emf. Lenz\'s law fixes the direction, always opposing the change. **Common mistake:** Expecting a current while the magnet sits still inside the coil.',
  'SCI-10-12|Generators':
    '**What it is:** A machine that turns movement into electricity by spinning a coil in a magnetic field. **Why it works:** Rotation keeps changing the flux through the coil, so an emf is induced continuously and reverses every half turn, giving alternating current; the turbine\'s mechanical energy becomes electrical energy. **Common mistake:** Saying it converts electrical energy into mechanical energy - that is what a motor does.',
  'SCI-10-12|Right-Hand Thumb Rule':
    '**What it is:** The rule for the direction of the magnetic field around a straight current-carrying wire. **Why it works:** Grip the wire in your right hand with the thumb along the current, and the curled fingers show the field\'s circular direction around the conductor; reverse the current and the circles reverse. **Common mistake:** Using the left hand, or pointing the fingers along the wire instead of using the thumb.',
  'SCI-10-13|Biogeochemical Cycles':
    '**What it is:** The looping paths by which carbon, nitrogen and other elements move between living things and the earth. **Why it works:** Matter is reused, so carbon returns to the air through respiration and decomposition while nitrogen is fixed into nitrates plants can absorb, linking atmosphere, organisms, soil and oceans. **Common mistake:** Thinking nutrients appear from nowhere - matter is recycled, only energy flows one way.',
  'SCI-10-13|Global Warming':
    '**What it is:** The slow rise in the earth\'s average temperature caused by heat that cannot escape. **Why it works:** Carbon dioxide and methane let sunlight in but hold back the heat radiated from the surface, so burning more fossil fuels traps more heat, melts polar ice and raises sea level. **Common mistake:** Blaming the ozone hole for warming, or naming oxygen as a greenhouse gas.',
  'SCI-10-13|Ozone Depletion':
    '**What it is:** The thinning of the ozone layer high in the stratosphere. **Why it works:** Ozone absorbs ultraviolet radiation, so losing it lets more UV reach the ground and damage skin and crops; chlorofluorocarbons release chlorine, and one chlorine atom destroys many ozone molecules. **Common mistake:** Confusing the ozone hole with the greenhouse effect - one lets UV in, the other traps heat.',
  'SCI-10-2|Neutralisation':
    '**What it is:** The reaction in which an acid and a base cancel each other out. **Why it works:** The H+ from the acid and the OH- from the base join to form water, leaving the metal ion and the acid radical to pair up as a salt: NaOH + HCl -> NaCl + H2O. **Common mistake:** Leaving out the water, or pairing the ions the wrong way round and naming the wrong salt.',
  'SCI-10-2|Strong and Weak Acids':
    '**What it is:** How completely an acid releases hydrogen ions once it is dissolved in water. **Why it works:** A strong acid ionises almost fully, giving many H+ ions and a low pH, while a weak acid such as acetic acid ionises only partly. Dry hydrogen chloride cannot change dry litmus because the ions appear only in water. **Common mistake:** Judging strength by concentration or by how corrosive it feels instead of by how much it ionises.',
  'SCI-10-3|Displacement':
    '**What it is:** A reaction in which a more reactive metal takes the place of a less reactive one in a compound. **Why it works:** Reactivity is the deciding rule, so iron in copper sulphate pushes copper out: the nail gains a brown coating and the blue solution fades towards pale green as FeSO4 forms. **Common mistake:** Expecting the less reactive metal to displace the more reactive one, or calling the colour change mere rusting.',
  'SCI-10-3|Hardness':
    '**What it is:** How easily a solid\'s surface can be scratched, a property that separates metals sharply. **Why it works:** Bonding decides it: sodium\'s loosely held atoms cut with a knife, while in diamond each carbon is held to four others in a rigid network, making it the hardest natural substance. **Common mistake:** Assuming every metal is hard, or confusing hardness with a metal\'s shine.',
  'SCI-10-4|Hydrocarbons':
    '**What it is:** Compounds made only of carbon and hydrogen, sorted by how their carbons are bonded. **Why it works:** Saturated ones have only single bonds - the alkanes, general formula CnH2n+2, so ethane is C2H6 - while one double bond makes an alkene unsaturated, as in ethene C2H4. **Common mistake:** Calling ethene saturated, or using CnH2n+2 to write the formula of an alkene.',
  'SCI-10-4|Structures':
    '**What it is:** The way a molecule\'s atoms are actually joined, which its formula alone never shows. **Why it works:** Carbon makes four covalent bonds, so methane has four C-H bonds, and one formula can be drawn in different ways, which is what structural isomers are. A ring changes the formula too: cyclohexane is C6H12, not C6H14. **Common mistake:** Thinking the formula fixes the structure, or counting only three bonds round methane\'s carbon.',
  'SCI-10-5|Blood Vessels':
    '**What it is:** The three kinds of tube that carry blood - arteries, veins and capillaries. **Why it works:** Arteries take blood away from the heart under high pressure, so their walls are thick and elastic; capillaries are one cell thin so materials pass into the tissues; veins return blood at low pressure and have valves. **Common mistake:** Saying arteries always carry oxygenated blood - the pulmonary artery carries deoxygenated blood.',
  'SCI-10-5|Digestion':
    '**What it is:** Breaking food down into molecules small enough to be absorbed into the blood. **Why it works:** It begins in the mouth, where an enzyme in saliva acts on starch; in the small intestine bile from the liver emulsifies fats into tiny droplets so enzymes can finish the job. Urea, by contrast, is made in the liver. **Common mistake:** Saying bile digests fat - it only breaks fat into droplets for the enzymes.',
  'SCI-10-6|Endocrine Glands':
    '**What it is:** Ductless glands that release hormones straight into the blood to control body functions. **Why it works:** Each hormone acts on particular cells, so the pancreas makes insulin to lower blood sugar, the thyroid needs iodine to make thyroxin, and the pituitary and adrenals govern growth and response. **Common mistake:** Saying iodine makes thyroxin; iodine is only needed to synthesise it, and the thyroid gland produces it.',
  'SCI-10-6|Feedback Mechanism':
    '**What it is:** The loop by which the body senses a change and corrects it back towards normal. **Why it works:** The useful kind is negative feedback: rising blood sugar makes the pancreas release insulin, which pulls the level down, and falling sugar reduces insulin so it can rise again - much like a thermostat. **Common mistake:** Thinking feedback pushes change further; in the body it works to oppose the change.',
  'SCI-10-6|Reflex Arc':
    '**What it is:** The short, fixed route a nerve impulse takes for a fast response, without waiting for the brain. **Why it works:** The signal runs receptor, sensory neuron, spinal cord, motor neuron, effector, so your hand pulls back before you feel the pain - speed matters more here than thinking. **Common mistake:** Putting the brain in the middle of the path, which would defeat the point of being fast.',
  'SCI-10-7|Budding':
    '**What it is:** A new individual growing out of the parent\'s body as a small outgrowth that later separates. **Why it works:** Repeated cell division at one spot makes the bud, which matures and detaches - yeast does it on a tiny scale, and Hydra grows a bump on its side that develops tentacles and drops off. **Common mistake:** Confusing it with binary fission, where the parent splits into two equal halves.',
  'SCI-10-7|Flower Parts':
    '**What it is:** The four sets of a flower, two of which do the reproducing. **Why it works:** The stamen is male, its anther making pollen on a filament; the pistil is female, with stigma, style and ovary holding the ovules, while sepals protect the bud and petals attract visitors. **Common mistake:** Saying the anther is the male part - it is only the pollen sac of the stamen.',
  'SCI-10-7|Pollination':
    '**What it is:** The transfer of pollen from the anther to the stigma, without which fertilisation cannot begin. **Why it works:** Agents carry it - wind, water, insects, birds - so pollen landing on the same flower\'s stigma is self-pollination and pollen reaching another flower is cross-pollination; net the flowers and no pumpkin forms. **Common mistake:** Thinking pollination is fertilisation; pollen must land, then grow a tube to the ovule.',
  'SCI-10-8|Natural Selection':
    '**What it is:** Darwin\'s mechanism, in which the surroundings keep the variations that help survival and reproduction. **Why it works:** Individuals vary, far more young are born than can survive, and those better suited live to pass the useful trait on, so the population shifts over generations. **Common mistake:** Thinking an animal can choose to adapt in its lifetime and pass that on; only inherited variation is selected.',
  'SCI-10-8|Punnett Square':
    '**What it is:** A grid that predicts the offspring of a cross from the parents\' alleles. **Why it works:** Put one parent\'s two alleles across the top and the other\'s down the side, then fill the four boxes: Tt x Tt gives TT, Tt, Tt, tt, so one quarter are short, while TT x tt gives all Tt and all tall. **Common mistake:** Thinking dominant means the more common trait, or that 3:1 means exactly three plants in every four.',
  'SCI-10-8|Speciation':
    '**What it is:** The splitting of one species into two groups that can no longer interbreed. **Why it works:** Populations separated by a barrier collect different variations; when they meet again they cannot produce fertile offspring, and that reproductive isolation has made them separate species. **Common mistake:** Calling any visible difference a new species - the test is whether they can still breed together.',
  'SCI-10-9|Image Formation':
    '**What it is:** Where and what kind of picture a curved mirror makes, decided by where the object stands. **Why it works:** Beyond the focus a concave mirror gives a real, inverted image, diminished too when the object is past the centre of curvature; between the pole and the focus it gives a virtual, erect, enlarged one. **Common mistake:** Assuming a concave mirror always makes a real image, when an object inside the focus gives a virtual one.',
  'SCI-10-9|Lenses and Mirrors':
    '**What it is:** The two kinds of lens, convex and concave, and the way each bends light. **Why it works:** A convex lens converges rays to a real focus, so it is the converging lens; a concave lens diverges them and always gives a virtual, erect, diminished image. Power = 1/f in metres, so 50 cm gives 1/0.5 = +2.0 D. **Common mistake:** Leaving f in centimetres in the power formula, or expecting a concave lens to magnify.',
  'ENG-9-1|Bharat Our Land':
    '**What it is:** A poem that speaks to India as a mother whose rivers, mountains and soil are her own body. **Why it works:** Calling the land a mother turns scenery into family - the mighty Himavant has no equal on earth, and the rivers run from her like limbs - so love of country becomes love of a parent. **Common mistake:** Reading it as plain geography and missing that one shared land and heritage are what unite the people.',
  'ENG-9-1|Learning at Any Age':
    '**What it is:** A sixty-two-year-old grandmother decides to learn to read and takes her twelve-year-old granddaughter as her teacher. **Why it works:** She practises at night, learns the letters by heart and soon reads a novel on her own, then touches her teacher\'s feet to show that learning outranks age and family rank. **Common mistake:** Thinking the story praises the child\'s kindness, when the point is the grandmother\'s determination.',
  'ENG-9-1|Patriotic Imagery':
    '**What it is:** The pictures a poet picks - rivers and mountains spoken of as a mother\'s limbs, the Ganga praised for purity and generosity - so the country feels loved. **Why it works:** An image reaches the senses before the mind, so naming a river a mother\'s arm turns a map into a body you belong to. **Common mistake:** Listing the images as decoration without saying what feeling they stir in the reader.',
  'ENG-9-2|Craftsmanship and Vocation':
    '**What it is:** A vocation is work a person feels called to do; craftsmanship is the skill, patience and care with which it is shaped. **Why it works:** The potter repeats one motion until the clay holds its form, so the worth lies in the doing, not only the sale - which is why a better-paying trade does not make potting worthless. **Common mistake:** Treating a trade as a mere job for money and ignoring the calling inside it.',
  'ENG-9-2|Dignity of Labour':
    '**What it is:** The belief that every honest kind of work deserves respect, whoever does it and however little it pays. **Why it works:** The care and meaning inside a pot maker\'s craft show the work as an expression of the person - the voice of a vocation is the voice of identity - so honouring the craft honours the worker. **Common mistake:** Assuming dignity belongs only to clean or well-paid jobs and looking down on work done with the hands.',
  'ENG-9-2|Gifts of Grace: Honouring Our Vocations':
    '**What it is:** A poem that hears the whole country celebrating its trades - artisans, carpenters, boatmen, cooks and masons - each proud of what belongs to it alone. **Why it works:** The line about hearing Bharat celebrating opens and closes the poem, so the nation becomes one chorus of workers and each craft is honoured as a gift passed down. **Common mistake:** Treating that repeated line as carelessness, when it frames the celebration.',
  'ENG-9-3|Canvas of Soil':
    '**What it is:** A way of seeing a garden bed as a painter\'s canvas, with the soil as the surface and the plants as the picture on it. **Why it works:** Seeds planted in careful rows are the first brushstrokes, so the closing line - gardens become paintings still - means both that the picture goes on and that it is a still life. **Common mistake:** Missing the pun on still and reading the line as saying the garden has stopped changing.',
  'ENG-9-3|Gardening as Art':
    '**What it is:** The claim that making a garden is an art, not just a chore, because the gardener arranges colours and shapes as a painter does. **Why it works:** Both choose a design, wait patiently and judge by beauty; brushstrokes of seeds planted true make sowing the gardener\'s first stroke on the canvas of soil. **Common mistake:** Believing art must be permanent or made of paint, so denying that a living garden can be art.',
  'ENG-9-3|Heritage and Change':
    '**What it is:** The pressure old crafts face when times change - the winds of change - and how a tradition can survive it. **Why it works:** A craft survives by adapting, not freezing: new designs and new uses for old skills, and first of all by celebrating the pankha\'s culture and artistry so people value it enough to keep it. **Common mistake:** Believing that preserving heritage means keeping it exactly as it was and refusing new ideas.',
  'ENG-9-4|Dignity and Independence':
    '**What it is:** The right of an older person to keep deciding for themselves, and the care that protects it instead of removing it. **Why it works:** Independence keeps self-respect alive, so help works best offered only as needed and after asking; Grandpa, seventy-five, resents being forbidden to go out, which treats him like a baby. **Common mistake:** Thinking loving care means deciding for an elder rather than with them.',
  'ENG-9-4|I Cannot Remember My Mother':
    '**What it is:** A poem where a child says he cannot remember his mother, yet every line circles what she left: a tune over his playthings, the scent of shiuli flowers, her gaze. **Why it works:** Her face is lost, but she survives through the senses, so the refrain measures rather than denies the memory: what remains of her fills the sky. **Common mistake:** Reading the line as proof he feels nothing, when it is the source of his grief.',
  'ENG-9-4|Sensory Memory and Absence':
    '**What it is:** Memory that arrives through a smell, a sound or a touch rather than through facts - here the shiuli flowers and the scent of the morning temple service. **Why it works:** One sense can bring a person back whole, so an absent mother returns as scent and tune, and the quiet grief stays mixed with tenderness. **Common mistake:** Hunting for a description of her face, and missing that the feeling is built from sensory detail alone.',
  'ENG-9-5|Empathy, Sympathy and Compassion':
    '**What it is:** Three responses to another\'s trouble - empathy is feeling with them, sympathy is feeling for them, and compassion is doing something without being asked. **Why it works:** Feeling with someone moves you to act, which is why Dr Malik asks for dignity and fair chances for disabled people rather than pity: pity looks down, dignity treats them as equals. **Common mistake:** Calling sympathy compassion, when compassion needs action.',
  'ENG-9-5|Nine Gold Medals':
    '**What it is:** A poem about a race where the smallest runner stumbles and the other eight turn back, lift him and finish together, so all nine get gold. **Why it works:** The officials honour what the runners did, not who came first, which is why the Special Olympics banner could not have been more on the mark: kindness was the real event. **Common mistake:** Assuming the medal was a consolation prize for losing, when it rewarded helping.',
  'ENG-9-5|Solidarity over Competition':
    '**What it is:** The choice to stand with others rather than beat them - the poem\'s claim that human kindness matters more than coming first. **Why it works:** In Nine Gold Medals every runner gives up a sure win to lift one fallen athlete, so the result stops measuring speed and starts measuring loyalty. **Common mistake:** Thinking solidarity means never trying to win, when it means refusing to win at someone else\'s expense.',
  'ENG-9-6|A Friend Found in Music':
    '**What it is:** The idea that music can be a companion - something that stays with you, comforts you when you are alone and asks nothing back. **Why it works:** The poet pictures music as the ocean pulling her to the shore, so it keeps company in an empty room; making it yourself, though, takes years of patient practice. **Common mistake:** Treating music as only entertainment and so missing the friendship and comfort it offers a lonely person.',
  'ENG-9-6|Music as Consolation':
    '**What it is:** The comfort music gives a sad person - not a distraction but a hand that holds you until the bad time passes. **Why it works:** A raga can hold you like a hand, and music lifting your spirits to make sure you pull through means more than cheerfulness: the lift carries you to the other side, which is why it is called a therapy. **Common mistake:** Reading pull through as feeling better for a while, when it means surviving.',
  'ENG-9-6|Play: Tradition Meets Innovation':
    '**What it is:** Where an old musical form meets a new one - classical training and Western tunes played together. **Why it works:** Shruti hides the fusion rehearsals from her father, fearing he will call it a desecration; yet setting the traditions side by side makes twin melodies, and innovation gives the old form new ears. **Common mistake:** Assuming tradition and innovation are enemies, when the old form survives by being played anew.',
  'ENG-9-7|Few Words, How Dear':
    '**What it is:** The thought that a few true words reach further than a flood of fine-sounding ones. **Why it works:** Hosts of words only reach the head, while a little said and truly said imparts deeper joy because it touches the heart - so the voice that cheers a lonely home is dear precisely because it has the fewest words to say. **Common mistake:** Judging a message by its length or cleverness instead of by how sincerely it is meant.',
  'ENG-9-7|Words':
    '**What it is:** The power of words to comfort, unite and heal - and their lightness, since once spoken they cannot be called back. **Why it works:** Swain compares words to summer birds because they fly off and leave only empty air behind, so a kind word given now is the whole gift; a letter with good news is welcome for the same reason. **Common mistake:** Believing words cost nothing and can be taken back, when their effect outlives the sound.',
  'ENG-9-7|Words, Trust and Service':
    '**What it is:** The postman\'s quiet duty - carrying other people\'s words faithfully in all weather, and keeping confidence in what he carries. **Why it works:** Villagers trust Khetaram because he never fails them, and that trust is why a letter with a torn corner is read aloud twice and then destroyed rather than let half a message spread. **Common mistake:** Thinking the postman matters only for speed of delivery, not for the trust he carries.',
  'ENG-9-8|Believe in Yourself':
    '**What it is:** The poem\'s argument that the decision to move towards your future is made alone, with no crowd watching and no applause. **Why it works:** It is just you and the future and where you want to be, so self-belief must be its own witness; that is what lets you attempt hard things and try again after failing. **Common mistake:** Waiting for someone else\'s approval before starting, and reading the empty field as a reason to stay.',
  'ENG-9-8|Letter from a Mother':
    '**What it is:** A parent\'s letter urging her child never to give up, and putting a number on the patience a serious goal demands. **Why it works:** The letter of 19 June 1995 sets the standard at least ten years of sustained practice to reach world-class level in any field, so early struggle is normal; coming from a parent it also carries the support that keeps you going. **Common mistake:** Reading the ten years as a reason to quit.',
  'ENG-9-8|The First Step Is the Hardest':
    '**What it is:** The claim that beginning costs more courage than continuing, because it breaks with the way things already are. **Why it works:** To start you must leave the ease of comfort and the status quo and act without proof it will work; after that each step has the one behind it, and breaking a large goal into small actions shrinks that first step. **Common mistake:** Believing the difficulty lies in talent, when it lies in starting.',
  'MAT-9-1|Coordinate Geometry in History and Navigation':
    '**What it is:** How people learned to fix a place with numbers, from sailors to graphs of points. **Why it works:** Once a position is an ordered pair it can be calculated with, so the astrolabe\'s idea also finds distances; Rene Descartes is credited with coordinate geometry, and ancient geography measured longitude from Ujjayini. **Common mistake:** Assuming coordinates are a modern invention with no navigational past.',
  'MAT-9-1|Quadrants and the Signs of Coordinates':
    '**What it is:** The four regions the two axes cut the plane into, each with its own pattern of plus and minus signs for x and y. **Why it works:** Count anticlockwise from the top right: I is (+,+), II is (-,+), III is (-,-) and IV is (+,-), so (-3, 5) has a negative x and a positive y and sits in the second quadrant. **Common mistake:** Counting clockwise, or reading the y sign first, which sends (-7, 5) to the third quadrant.',
  'MAT-9-1|The Distance Formula':
    '**What it is:** A rule for the straight-line gap between two points, made by turning that gap into the hypotenuse of a right triangle. **Why it works:** The x-gap and y-gap are its legs, so distance = root of ((x2-x1)^2 + (y2-y1)^2) by the Baudhayana-Pythagoras theorem: from (1, 2) to (4, 6) the gaps are 3 and 4, giving 5. **Common mistake:** Subtracting in the wrong order and losing a sign, or adding the gaps instead of squaring and rooting.',
  'MAT-9-2|Linear Growth, Decay and Linear Relationships':
    '**What it is:** A quantity that changes by the same amount every step - growing like a fare with a fixed charge per km, or decaying like a draining tank. **Why it works:** Start plus or minus rate times steps gives the expression: 50 rupees plus 10 per km is 50 + 10k, and 200 litres draining 20 a minute is 200 - 20m. **Common mistake:** Making the rate the constant term and the start the coefficient, which reverses growth and decay.',
  'MAT-9-2|Linear Polynomials and the Degree of a Polynomial':
    '**What it is:** A polynomial whose highest power of the variable is 1, so its graph is straight, such as 3x + 2; the degree is that highest power. **Why it works:** The degree counts the largest exponent on the variable, not the size of a coefficient: 3z + 7 has degree 1, while 5 - 2x^2 + x^4 has degree 4 because x^4 is the biggest power. **Common mistake:** Calling the biggest coefficient the degree, or counting a constant as having a power.',
  'MAT-9-2|Visualising Linear Relationships with Graphs':
    '**What it is:** The picture a linear relationship makes on a graph - always a straight line, because the change is steady. **Why it works:** Every extra unit of x adds the same amount to y, so the points never curve: for y = 2x + 1, x = 3 gives y = 7, and (0,1), (1,3), (2,5) lie on one line whose slope is the rate. **Common mistake:** Expecting a curve for a linear rule, or plotting a few points and joining them without a table.',
  'MAT-9-3|Integers, Fractions and the Density of Rational Numbers':
    '**What it is:** The fact that between any two rational numbers you can always fit another, so no rational has a next one. **Why it works:** Average the two and you land between them - halfway between 1/3 and 1/2 is 5/12 - and the trick repeats for ever, so infinitely many rationals lie in that gap, unlike 1 and 2 among whole numbers. **Common mistake:** Assuming a largest rational exists below 1/2, or expecting a finite count between fractions.',
  'MAT-9-3|Irrational Numbers, Decimals and Cyclic Numbers':
    '**What it is:** Numbers that cannot be written as a fraction of two integers, so their decimals never end and never repeat. **Why it works:** Any terminating or repeating decimal turns back into a fraction, so whatever cannot is irrational: the square root of 2 and pi are examples, while 0.142857142857... repeats 142857 for ever. **Common mistake:** Calling a long or messy decimal irrational, when a repeating pattern makes it rational.',
  'MAT-9-3|Zero, the Bakhshali Manuscript and Brahmagupta’s Rules':
    '**What it is:** Zero as a number with its own rules, first written as a bold dot in the Bakhshali Manuscript and formalised with negatives by Brahmagupta. **Why it works:** Once zero had rules it could be calculated with: anything times zero is zero, adding zero changes nothing, so (-9) x 0 + 0 - (-4) is 0 + 0 + 4 = 4, because subtracting a negative adds. **Common mistake:** Missing the double negative at the end and answering -4.',
  'MAT-9-4|Factorisation Using Identities and Algebra Tiles':
    '**What it is:** Writing a polynomial as a product of simpler expressions by spotting the pattern of a standard identity. **Why it works:** Match a^2 + 2ab + b^2 or a^2 - b^2 and the factors fall out: x^2 + 6x + 9 is (x+3)^2 since 2 times x times 3 is 6x, and x^2 - 9 is (x-3)(x+3) since 9 is 3^2; for x^2 - 5x + 6 use two numbers multiplying to 6 and adding to -5. **Common mistake:** Ignoring the middle term, so 6x is never checked against 2ab.',
  'MAT-9-4|Simplifying Rational Expressions':
    '**What it is:** Reducing a fraction made of polynomials to its simplest form by removing whatever the top and bottom share. **Why it works:** Factorise both parts first, then cancel a common factor and never a term: (x^2 - 4)/(x - 2) becomes (x-2)(x+2)/(x-2), and cancelling (x-2) leaves x + 2, valid unless x is 2. **Common mistake:** Cancelling an x from x^2 and from x separately, which is not a common factor and gives a wrong answer.',
  'MAT-9-4|Squares and Rectangles as Visual Proofs':
    '**What it is:** Showing an identity as an area, by cutting a big square of side (a + b) into four pieces. **Why it works:** The pieces are squares of a^2 and b^2 plus two rectangles each of area ab, so the total a^2 + 2ab + b^2 proves (a+b)^2; with a = 4 and b = 3 the two rectangles hold 2 x 12 = 24. **Common mistake:** Counting the two rectangles once instead of twice, which silently drops the 2ab term.',
  'MAT-9-5|Circle, Centre, Radius and Chord':
    '**What it is:** The fixed middle point is the centre; the radius runs from it to the circle, and a chord joins any two points on the circle. **Why it works:** A chord through the centre is a diameter, made of two radii, so the diameter is twice the radius and the longest chord of a circle of radius 10 cm is 20 cm. **Common mistake:** Calling the radius a chord, or thinking every chord must pass through the centre.',
  'MAT-9-5|Perpendicular Bisector of a Chord':
    '**What it is:** The line that cuts a chord at right angles through its midpoint - in a circle it always passes through the centre. **Why it works:** Two radii and half the chord form a right triangle, so the perpendicular from the centre bisects it: a 16 cm chord is 8 cm in half, 6 cm from the centre, so radius = root of (8^2 + 6^2) = 10 cm. **Common mistake:** Putting the whole chord into the triangle instead of half of it.',
  'MAT-9-5|Symmetries of a Circle':
    '**What it is:** The symmetry of a shape that looks the same when folded or turned - and a circle has more of it than any other plane shape. **Why it works:** Every diameter is a line of symmetry, and infinitely many diameters exist; it also turns onto itself through any angle, so its rotational symmetry has infinite order, all of it about the centre. **Common mistake:** Saying a circle has four lines of symmetry by confusing it with a square.',
  'MAT-9-6|Area of a Triangle and Heron’s Formula':
    '**What it is:** A way of finding a triangle\'s area from its three sides alone, with no height needed. **Why it works:** Add the sides and halve for s, then area = root of (s(s-a)(s-b)(s-c)): for 13, 14 and 15, s = 21 and 21 x 8 x 7 x 6 = 7056, so the area is 84 cm^2; for 10, 10 and 12, s = 16, giving 48 cm^2. **Common mistake:** Using the full perimeter instead of the semi-perimeter s, which spoils every bracket.',
  'MAT-9-6|Perimeter of a Shape':
    '**What it is:** The total length of a shape\'s boundary - the distance walked once round it - in units of length, never square units. **Why it works:** Add the sides: a square of side 6 cm has perimeter 24 cm. Equal perimeters do not mean equal areas - the square encloses 36 cm^2, while a rectangle of 8 cm by 4 cm with the same perimeter encloses only 32. **Common mistake:** Writing cm^2 for perimeter, or assuming equal perimeter means equal area.',
  'MAT-9-6|The C/D Ratio and Circumference of a Circle':
    '**What it is:** For every circle the circumference divided by the diameter gives the same number, pi, so circumference is pi times diameter. **Why it works:** Because the ratio is constant, one circle tells you about all: with pi as 22/7 and radius 7 cm the diameter is 14 cm, so the circumference is (22/7) x 14 = 44 cm, since C = 2 x pi x r. **Common mistake:** Putting the radius where the diameter belongs, which gives 22 cm, not 44.',
  'MAT-9-7|Sample Space and Events':
    '**What it is:** The sample space lists every possible outcome of a trial, and an event is the set of outcomes you care about. **Why it works:** Listing the space makes counting honest: one die gives 6 outcomes, two coins give 4 - HH, HT, TH, TT - because each coin doubles the list, and an event is then a part of that list. **Common mistake:** Listing only three outcomes for two coins by treating HT and TH as the same, so the four look unequal.',
  'MAT-9-7|Tree Diagrams':
    '**What it is:** A branching picture listing every combination of outcomes when two or more things happen in turn. **Why it works:** Each event adds its own branches and you multiply: a coin gives 2 and a die 6, so 2 x 6 = 12 outcomes, and only (H, 6) is favourable, giving a probability of 1/12. **Common mistake:** Adding the branches, 2 + 6 = 8, instead of multiplying them.',
  'MAT-9-7|What is Probability?':
    '**What it is:** A number from 0 to 1 measuring how likely an event is, where 0 is impossible and 1 is certain. **Why it works:** For equally likely outcomes it is favourable outcomes divided by all outcomes, so a fair coin gives 1/2 for a head; since favourable outcomes can never exceed the total, an impossible event scores 0 and a certain one 1. **Common mistake:** Giving a probability above 1 or below 0, or calling a 0.5 chance certain.',
  'MAT-9-8|Explicit and Recursive Rules':
    '**What it is:** Two ways to describe a sequence - an explicit rule gives any term from its position number n, while a recursive rule builds each term from the previous one. **Why it works:** With t(n) = 3n - 2 you jump anywhere: the 10th term is 3 x 10 - 2 = 28, needing no earlier term. A rule such as add 4 makes you step through the terms one by one. **Common mistake:** Forgetting that n starts at 1.',
  'MAT-9-8|Geometric Progressions':
    '**What it is:** A sequence in which each term is the previous one times the same fixed number, the common ratio. **Why it works:** Divide any term by the previous one to find it: 2, 6, 18, 54 multiplies by 3, so the next term is 162. Ratios also let you skip ahead, since multiplying by r twice gives r^2. **Common mistake:** Adding a constant instead of multiplying, which makes it arithmetic, not geometric.',
  'MAT-9-8|Introduction to Sequences':
    '**What it is:** An ordered list of numbers following a rule, where the order matters and the position number n says which term you have. **Why it works:** Find the rule, then extend it: 3, 7, 11, 15 adds 4 so the next term is 19, while 2, 6, 18, 54 multiplies by 3 and goes to 162; checking two steps confirms the rule before you commit. **Common mistake:** Assuming every list changes by a fixed difference, when the rule may multiply or square.',
  'SCI-9-1|Laws, Theories and Principles':
    '**What it is:** The kinds of statement science makes - a law says what happens every time it is tested, a theory explains why, and a principle is a guiding rule. **Why it works:** A law reports a pattern with no exception yet found, so it describes; a theory is an explanation built on evidence and testing, so it explains. **Common mistake:** Believing a theory is a guess that becomes a law once proved, when they answer different questions.',
  'SCI-9-1|Prediction, Reasoning and Estimation':
    '**What it is:** Saying what will happen before you measure, and roughly what number to expect, using evidence rather than hope. **Why it works:** A prediction is a reasoned expectation from laws, models and data, so it can be tested and shown wrong; an estimate first gives a rough value to judge the measurement against, so a wild answer signals an error. **Common mistake:** Treating a bare guess with no reasoning as a scientific prediction.',
  'SCI-9-1|Symbols, Units and Mathematics in Science':
    '**What it is:** The shorthand of science - one agreed letter for each quantity and one agreed unit, so numbers compare and equations stay short. **Why it works:** A shared unit means a mass measured in Delhi and Tokyo means the same without conversion, and a symbol keeps equations brief: c stands for the speed of light because of celeritas. **Common mistake:** Writing a number without its unit, or guessing a symbol comes from English.',
  'SCI-9-10|Echo, Reverberation and Ultrasonic Waves':
    '**What it is:** A reflected sound heard separately is an echo, a reflection that lingers and blurs is reverberation, and sound above human hearing is ultrasonic. **Why it works:** Sound reflects off hard surfaces and must travel there and back, so a 0.5 s delay means 340 x 0.5 / 2 = 85 m to the wall; reverberation is the same reflection arriving too soon to separate. **Common mistake:** Forgetting the return journey and reporting 170 m.',
  'SCI-9-10|Production of Sound and Tuning Fork':
    '**What it is:** Sound is made whenever a body vibrates - a tuning fork being a U-shaped steel bar whose two prongs, or tines, vibrate rapidly when struck. **Why it works:** The prongs push the air into compressions and rarefactions that reach your ear, so sound lasts only while they vibrate; a bigger vibration is louder and a faster one higher in pitch. **Common mistake:** Thinking the fork sounds only because it is struck.',
  'SCI-9-10|Sound Needs a Medium to Propagate':
    '**What it is:** Sound cannot cross empty space: it must travel through a material medium, whether solid, liquid or gas. **Why it works:** Sound is a vibration passed from particle to particle, so with no particles nothing passes it on: in the bell jar the ringing fades as air is pumped out, and the Moon has no air, so no sound; particles sit closest in solids, so sound is fastest there. **Common mistake:** Believing sound travels fastest in air.',
  'SCI-9-11|Asexual Reproduction':
    '**What it is:** Making new individuals from one parent alone, with no gametes and no fertilisation, so the offspring are genetically identical to it. **Why it works:** A single cell or body part divides or buds off and grows into a whole organism - a Hydra grows a bud that separates, a potato sprouts from an eye - so one set of genes means speed but no variation. **Common mistake:** Expecting the offspring to differ from the parent.',
  'SCI-9-11|Gametogenesis and Human Reproduction':
    '**What it is:** The making of gametes - sperm and egg - and their fusion in fertilisation, which begins a new individual. **Why it works:** Gametes form by meiosis, which halves the chromosome number, so a human sperm or egg carries 23 while a body cell carries 46; at fertilisation the sets join and the zygote has 46 again in 23 pairs. **Common mistake:** Thinking a gamete carries 46 chromosomes, or that fertilisation joins two body cells.',
  'SCI-9-11|Vegetative Propagation':
    '**What it is:** Growing a new plant from a part of the parent - a root, a stem or a leaf - rather than from a seed. **Why it works:** The new plant is a clone carrying exactly the parent\'s qualities, which is why farmers use the method for sugarcane; layering buries the middle of a flexible twig until roots form. **Common mistake:** Assuming the new plant will show variation, or believing every plant part is equally usable.',
  'SCI-9-12|Binomial Nomenclature and Biodiversity Under Threat':
    '**What it is:** The two-word naming system for living things, genus then species, and the biodiversity now being lost. **Why it works:** One shared name removes confusion between scientists, and the rules are fixed: genus capitalised, species small, both italic - Panthera leo, not Panthera Leo; the main threat to biodiversity is habitat loss. **Common mistake:** Capitalising the species name, or thinking the first word names the species.',
  'SCI-9-12|India as a Biodiversity Hotspot':
    '**What it is:** A region packed with species found nowhere else and under threat, which India is thanks to its many climate zones and habitats. **Why it works:** Mountains, coasts, deserts and rainforests in one country make many separate niches, and species evolve apart and become endemic, so losing a habitat is costly. **Common mistake:** Calling any species-rich country a hotspot, forgetting that being under threat is part of the definition.',
  'SCI-9-12|Need for Classification':
    '**What it is:** Grouping living things by shared features so they can be studied, named and discussed in an orderly way. **Why it works:** The groups shrink from broad kingdom down to species, the smallest and most specific, so any organism can be placed and found again; the system shows relationships and gives every scientist one name for one thing. **Common mistake:** Thinking classification is only about naming, or placing species above genus.',
  'SCI-9-13|Human Impact on Earth\'s Processes':
    '**What it is:** The way human activity changes the cycles of air, water and life - the climate, rivers and lakes. **Why it works:** Burning fossil fuels adds carbon dioxide that traps heat, sewage poisons rivers, and fertiliser off fields loads a lake with nitrate; the algal bloom that follows dies, and its decay uses up dissolved oxygen, so the fish suffocate. **Common mistake:** Blaming the algae, when it is their decay that removes the oxygen.',
  'SCI-9-13|Uneven Heating of the Earth':
    '**What it is:** The uneven warming of the planet, because sunlight strikes the equator almost head-on but the poles at a slant. **Why it works:** The same beam spreads over a wider area near the poles, so less energy arrives per square metre; warm air rises at the equator and cooler air sinks elsewhere, and the pressure difference drives winds and ocean currents. **Common mistake:** Saying the poles are cold because they are farther from the Sun.',
  'SCI-9-13|Winds and Ocean Currents':
    '**What it is:** The large movements of air and water that carry heat around the planet, driven by uneven heating. **Why it works:** Warm air rises and cooler air sinks, and air flows from high to low pressure; the Earth\'s rotation then bends that path, so Northern Hemisphere winds curve right. Ocean currents carry heat from warmer to cooler regions. **Common mistake:** Believing rotation causes the winds, when it only bends them.',
  'SCI-9-2|Cell Membrane, Cell Wall and Cell Organelles':
    '**What it is:** The outer layer that controls what enters a cell, the rigid wall outside it in plants, and the structures inside. **Why it works:** The membrane lets some substances through and stops others, while the cell wall adds strength and shape; the mitochondrion releases energy and is called the powerhouse. **Common mistake:** Giving an animal cell a wall, or confusing the membrane\'s control with the wall\'s support.',
  'SCI-9-2|Cell Theory: The Unifying Principle of Biology':
    '**What it is:** The statements that all living things are made of cells, that the cell is the basic unit of life, and that new cells come from existing ones. **Why it works:** Because it holds for every organism, from bacterium to human, it unifies biology: one idea covers growth, repair and reproduction, all cells dividing. **Common mistake:** Expecting cell theory to list what is inside a cell, when it explains the unit of life.',
  'SCI-9-2|Mitosis, Meiosis and Cell Division':
    '**What it is:** Two kinds of cell division - mitosis makes two identical cells for growth and repair, meiosis makes gametes with half the chromosomes. **Why it works:** In mitosis the chromosomes are copied and shared equally, so each daughter matches the parent; meiosis divides twice, halving the number to 23 in humans, and fertilisation restores 46. **Common mistake:** Swapping the two and saying meiosis makes identical body cells.',
  'SCI-9-3|Epithelial, Connective, Muscular and Nervous Tissues':
    '**What it is:** The four animal tissues - epithelial for covering and protection, connective for joining and support, muscular for movement, nervous for messages. **Why it works:** Flat epithelial cells pack into a covering sheet, connective tissue includes tendons joining muscle to bone, muscle cells shorten, and nervous tissue is neurons. **Common mistake:** Saying tendons join bone to bone, which is a ligament.',
  'SCI-9-3|Permanent Tissues and Plant vs Animal Tissues':
    '**What it is:** Plant tissues that have stopped dividing and taken a fixed job, and how they differ from animal tissue. **Why it works:** A wall stiffened by lignin gives plant tissue strength: xylem carries water up the stem, while sclerenchyma, dead cells with thick walls, makes stems and seed coats hard. Animals have no cell wall, so they need bone. **Common mistake:** Assuming every plant cell is alive, when the hardest are dead.',
  'SCI-9-3|Types of Joints and the Skeletal System':
    '**What it is:** The places where bones meet, shaped so the body can move in particular ways, with the bones that support the body. **Why it works:** A hinge joint moves in one plane, like a door, as the elbow and knee do; a ball and socket joint lets a limb swing in every direction. The rib cage has 12 pairs of ribs, guarding the heart and lungs. **Common mistake:** Calling the elbow a ball and socket joint, or miscounting the pairs of ribs.',
  'SCI-9-4|Average Speed, Average Velocity and Acceleration':
    '**What it is:** Average speed is distance over time, average velocity is displacement over time, and acceleration is the change in velocity over the time taken. **Why it works:** Speed ignores direction, velocity does not, so a runner completing a 400 m lap in 40 s has average speed 10 m/s but average velocity 0; a change from 10 to 20 m/s in 5 s is 2 m/s^2. **Common mistake:** Using distance where velocity needs displacement.',
  'SCI-9-4|Kinematic Equations and Uniform Circular Motion':
    '**What it is:** The three equations for straight-line motion with constant acceleration, and a body circling at constant speed. **Why it works:** For u, v, a, t and s use v = u + at, s = ut + (1/2)at^2 and v^2 = u^2 + 2as, choosing the one that omits the unknown. In uniform circular motion the speed is fixed but the direction changes, so velocity changes. **Common mistake:** Concluding that circling at constant speed means no acceleration.',
  'SCI-9-4|Position-Time and Velocity-Time Graphs':
    '**What it is:** Graphs that turn motion into a shape - position against time, velocity against time - so the motion can be read off the picture. **Why it works:** On a distance-time graph the slope is speed, so a horizontal line means rest; on a velocity-time graph the slope is acceleration and the area under the line is the displacement. **Common mistake:** Reading a horizontal velocity-time line as rest, when it means steady velocity.',
  'SCI-9-5|Concentration and Solubility':
    '**What it is:** How much solute is dissolved in a given amount of solvent, and the most that will dissolve at a temperature - its solubility. **Why it works:** Mass by mass percentage is solute mass divided by the whole solution mass, times 100, so 10 g of salt in 90 g of water makes 100 g of solution and 10% m/m. **Common mistake:** Dividing by the water alone instead of the whole solution, which gives 11.1%.',
  'SCI-9-5|Crystallization, Distillation and Chromatography':
    '**What it is:** Three ways of separating a mixture filtration cannot handle - crystals, boiling point, and how far each travels on paper. **Why it works:** Crystallization recovers a pure solid by slow cooling; distillation separates miscible liquids whose boiling points differ by about 40 degrees C; chromatography separates because each sticks differently to solvent and paper. **Common mistake:** Distilling a solid dissolved in water.',
  'SCI-9-5|Immiscible Liquids, Sublimation, Suspensions and Colloids':
    '**What it is:** Mixtures where liquids refuse to mix, a solid turns straight into a gas, and particles settle out or stay invisibly small. **Why it works:** In a suspension the particles are visible and settle on standing, while in a colloid they stay dispersed and scatter a beam of light, the Tyndall effect; sublimation skips the liquid stage. **Common mistake:** Calling a colloid a true solution, or expecting it to settle out.',
  'SCI-9-6|Concept of Force':
    '**What it is:** A push or a pull that can change an object\'s speed, its direction or its shape. **Why it works:** Force is measured in newtons, and one newton gives a 1 kg mass an acceleration of 1 m/s^2; because force has direction as well as size, two equal pushes in opposite directions cancel to nothing. **Common mistake:** Thinking a force is needed to keep something moving steadily, when a net force is what changes motion.',
  'SCI-9-6|Force of Friction':
    '**What it is:** The force between surfaces in contact that resists sliding, acting opposite to motion. **Why it works:** Roughness makes surfaces catch, so friction turns motion into heat; oil forms a smooth film that reduces it. When a push cannot move a heavy box, friction grows until it balances the push, so the net force is zero and the box stays still. **Common mistake:** Believing the box feels no force when it does not move.',
  'SCI-9-6|Newton\'s First Law of Motion':
    '**What it is:** An object at rest stays at rest, and one in motion keeps a constant velocity, unless a net force acts - the law of inertia. **Why it works:** Matter resists change in its motion, so with zero net force nothing changes: a puck on frictionless ice keeps gliding straight at the same speed, and in a braking bus the body keeps its velocity and falls forward. **Common mistake:** Believing a constant force keeps something moving.',
  'SCI-9-7|Power':
    '**What it is:** The rate at which work is done - how fast energy is transferred - measured in watts, one joule per second. **Why it works:** Power is work divided by time, so the same work done faster means more power: two students carrying identical bags up the same stairs do equal work, but the one taking 1 minute instead of 5 has five times the power. **Common mistake:** Assuming more work means more power, when time matters too.',
  'SCI-9-7|Pulley, Inclined Plane and Lever':
    '**What it is:** Simple machines that let a smaller effort do a job - a pulley changes direction, a ramp spreads effort, a lever turns on a fulcrum. **Why it works:** A single fixed pulley only changes direction; a ramp trades a longer path for a smaller push; a crowbar with the fulcrum near the rock has an effort arm far longer than the load arm. **Common mistake:** Expecting a machine to reduce the work, when it only changes how.',
  'SCI-9-7|Work Done by a Constant Force':
    '**What it is:** Work is done when a force moves an object in the direction of the force, and it is measured in joules. **Why it works:** Work is force times the displacement along it, so 10 N moving a box 2 m does 20 J and through 3 m does 30 J; if the object does not move, or moves at right angles to the force, that force does no work however tired you feel. **Common mistake:** Counting work when someone pushes a wall that never moves.',
  'SCI-9-8|Atomic Number and Mass Number':
    '**What it is:** The atomic number counts the protons and fixes which element the atom is; the mass number counts protons and neutrons together. **Why it works:** In a neutral atom electrons equal protons, so atomic number 17 means 17 protons and 17 electrons, and mass number 35 leaves 35 - 17 = 18 neutrons. **Common mistake:** Adding electrons into the mass number, or treating it as counting only neutrons.',
  'SCI-9-8|Isotopes and Isobars':
    '**What it is:** Isotopes are atoms of the same element with different numbers of neutrons; isobars are atoms of different elements sharing a mass number. **Why it works:** Isotopes share the atomic number, so they behave chemically alike, and average mass is weighted over the mixture - chlorine is about 75% chlorine-35 and 25% chlorine-37, giving 35.5 u. **Common mistake:** Believing one atom can weigh 35.5 u, or swapping isobars for isotopes.',
  'SCI-9-8|Thomson\'s Model and the Gold Foil Experiment':
    '**What it is:** Two early pictures of the atom - Thomson\'s ball of positive charge, and Rutherford\'s test firing alpha particles at gold foil. **Why it works:** Almost all the particles passed straight through, but a few were deflected sharply and some bounced back, impossible if the charge were spread out; so the charge and mass sit in a tiny nucleus. **Common mistake:** Saying most particles were deflected, when only a few were.',
  'SCI-9-9|Law of Conservation of Mass':
    '**What it is:** In a reaction matter is only rearranged, never created or destroyed, so the products weigh exactly as much as the reactants. **Why it works:** Atoms are conserved and simply regroup, so in a closed container 4 g of hydrogen plus 32 g of oxygen gives exactly 36 g of water; the same balance is why an equation must show equal atoms on both sides. **Common mistake:** Forgetting that an escaping gas still has mass.',
  'SCI-9-9|Law of Constant Proportions':
    '**What it is:** A given compound always contains the same elements in the same fixed ratio by mass, however and wherever it was made. **Why it works:** Water is always hydrogen and oxygen in the mass ratio 1:8, from a river, a borewell or the ocean, because particles combine in a fixed whole-number ratio and each atom has a fixed mass. **Common mistake:** Expecting another source to give a different composition, or writing the ratio as 8:1.',
  'SCI-9-9|Molecular Mass and Formula Unit Mass':
    '**What it is:** The mass of a molecule, or of one formula unit of an ionic compound, found by adding the atomic masses of all its atoms. **Why it works:** Add every atom in the formula: carbon dioxide is 12 + 16 + 16 = 44 u, and Mg(OH)2 is 24 + 2 x (16 + 1) = 58 u, because the bracket doubles both the oxygen and the hydrogen. **Common mistake:** Forgetting to multiply inside the bracket, so Mg(OH)2 is worked out with one oxygen and one hydrogen.',
  'ENG-8-1|‘A Concrete Example’ by Reginald Arkell':
    '**What it is:** A light poem in which a speaker admires Mrs Jones\'s concrete garden and misses the treasure under his feet. **Why it works:** The title works as a pun - concrete is the paving stone and also a clear example - and her reply "You\'re standing on it" shows the lovely thing was a tiny plant between the stones. **Common mistake:** Taking the title only as building material and missing the joke about overlooking small things.',
  'ENG-8-1|Tenali Rama and the Paddy Seeds':
    '**What it is:** A court tale in which Tenali Rama sows the seeds the farmers were given and nothing grows. **Why it works:** A boiled seed is dead, so no care can make it sprout; when the courtiers blame lazy farmers, Tenali accepts the fault as the sower, using their own excuse, and makes them admit the seeds were spoiled. **Common mistake:** Thinking he really blamed the farmers, when he was turning their argument against them.',
  'ENG-8-1|The Play ‘Wisdom Paves the Way’ and Modals':
    '**What it is:** Modal verbs are helpers such as can, may, must, should and would that add ability, permission, advice or obligation to a main verb. **Why it works:** A modal never changes form and takes the plain verb, so "it is advisable for you to think" becomes "you should think", while must carries the strongest obligation or certainty. **Common mistake:** Adding -s or -ed to the modal or its verb, as in "he musts" or "she should thinks".',
  'ENG-8-2|‘Somebody’s Mother’ — Kindness and Empathy':
    '**What it is:** A poem in which a boy helps an old woman cross a slippery street and is thanked that night. **Why it works:** Calling her "somebody\'s mother" turns a stranger into someone owed the care he would want for his own mother, and the last stanza returns the phrase as "somebody\'s son", so the kindness comes full circle. **Common mistake:** Reading it only as a poem about old age, when every unknown person is somebody\'s beloved child.',
  'ENG-8-2|Subject–Verb Agreement, Dashes and Prepositional Phrases':
    '**What it is:** The rule that a verb must match its real subject in number, plus the punctuation that marks an added idea. **Why it works:** Words between subject and verb do not change it, so "a bouquet of flowers is" and "the list of names is" stay singular, since bouquet and list are the head words; a dash marks a pause before a surprise. **Common mistake:** Matching the verb to the nearest noun after of, giving "a bouquet of flowers are".',
  'ENG-8-2|Verghese Kurien — I Too Had a Dream':
    '**What it is:** The story of the man who built India\'s milk cooperatives. **Why it works:** He organised small farmers into a cooperative that owned the processing and kept the profit, so "I too had a dream" claims ordinary people can build something lasting; his letter says a life is well spent when talents serve the common good. **Common mistake:** Crediting him with a single factory or a private fortune, when he built a farmers\' business.',
  'ENG-8-3|‘Spectacular Wonders’ of India':
    '**What it is:** A chapter on India\'s wonders, from the Ajanta and Ellora rock-cut caves to the Lonar Crater Lake. **Why it works:** They record forces and skills that cannot be recreated - the caves were cut by hand into rock, and Lonar was formed in basaltic rock by a meteorite impact, the only such crater in India - so they must be protected. **Common mistake:** Assuming Lonar is a volcanic crater, when a meteorite strike made it.',
  'ENG-8-3|‘The Magic Brush of Dreams’ — a Folk Tale':
    '**What it is:** A folk tale about Gopi, whose brush brings her paintings to life, and who refuses a Zamindar. **Why it works:** She promised to paint only for the poor, so she answers "My lord, I can\'t comply"; the horse, road and river she paints free her, while the Zamindar gains nothing, so talent for others carries power. **Common mistake:** Believing the brush works for whoever owns it, when it serves only the painter who keeps the promise.',
  'ENG-8-3|Critical Thinking and Close Observation':
    '**What it is:** The habit of looking at small details patiently and testing each clue instead of trusting a confident claim. **Why it works:** A mystery is solved by evidence, so you compare what is said with what is there - Encyclopedia Brown asks whether a palm-tree nursery could hold a young fir, and that impossible detail exposes the lie. **Common mistake:** Believing the first explanation instead of testing each clue against the facts.',
  'ENG-8-4|‘Harvest Hymn’ by Sarojini Naidu':
    '**What it is:** A song of thanks at harvest, sung by the men, then the women, then everyone. **Why it works:** Each group names what it owes - the men praise Surya for light and Varuna for rain, the women praise Prithvi for her plentiful bosom, and all voices join for Bramha - so the plenty is shared work and gratitude. **Common mistake:** Reading it as praise of one god or one farmer, when it widens to sun, rain, earth and community.',
  'ENG-8-4|‘Waiting for the Rain’ by Kamakshi Balasubramanian':
    '**What it is:** A story of Velu and his village living through a long dry spell. **Why it works:** Crops and livelihoods depend on rain, so the waiting carries hope and anxiety at once; the old woman reads the delay as the land resting, since nature gives her children the rest they need, and that calm answer steadies the boy. **Common mistake:** Calling the mood simply sad, when the story keeps hope alive alongside the worry.',
  'ENG-8-4|Determiners, Adjectives and the Chipko Movement':
    '**What it is:** Determiners are words before a noun (a, the, this, those, little) that fix which or how much; adjectives describe it. **Why it works:** In "those tall trees", those fixes which trees and tall describes them; with an uncountable noun, little means hardly any, a little means some. Chipko was villagers hugging trees to save them. **Common mistake:** Using few with an uncountable noun such as hope, or taking those for an adjective.',
  'ENG-8-5|‘Magnifying Glass’ by Walter de la Mare':
    '**What it is:** A poem about a round glass that turns ordinary specks into a world of wonder. **Why it works:** The glass changes the scale of everything - an inch of moss becomes a forest, a drop of water a hive of bees - and the last stanza leaps to the moon, which with such lenses would seem close enough to walk to in an afternoon. **Common mistake:** Reading it as a poem about a toy, when curiosity about small things brings the distant near.',
  'ENG-8-5|Bibha Chowdhuri — The Beam of Light':
    '**What it is:** The life of a physicist who studied cosmic rays and subatomic particles when few women did research. **Why it works:** She used photographic plates to detect particle tracks and published pioneering work with modest equipment, and in 2019 the star HD 86081 in Leo was named Bibha, so her story shows obstacles need not stop good science. **Common mistake:** Assuming the recognition came easily, when the star was named decades later.',
  'ENG-8-5|Word Formation and the Active Voice':
    '**What it is:** Building words with prefixes and suffixes, and putting the doer first in a sentence. **Why it works:** A suffix such as -ist turns science into scientist; in the active voice the subject does the action: "The scientist measured the beam", not "The beam was measured by the scientist". **Common mistake:** Calling a passive sentence active because it mentions a person, or attaching -ist to words that do not take it.',
  'MAT-8-1|Patterns of Squares and Square Roots':
    '**What it is:** A square number is a number multiplied by itself, and its square root is the number that was multiplied. **Why it works:** 13 x 13 = 169, so the square root of 169 is 13 and the square root of 144 is 12; adding the first n odd numbers gives n squared, so 1 + 3 + 5 = 9 = 3 squared, which is a quick way to build squares. **Common mistake:** Treating square as double, so 13 squared is written as 26 instead of 169.',
  'MAT-8-1|Perfect Cubes and Cube Roots':
    '**What it is:** A perfect cube is what you get by multiplying a number by itself three times, and its cube root is that original number. **Why it works:** 4 x 4 x 4 = 64, so 64 is a perfect cube and its cube root is 4; 5 x 5 x 5 = 125, so the cube root of 125 is 5, and you check the answer by cubing it back to 125. **Common mistake:** Confusing cubing with multiplying by 3, so 4 cubed is written as 12, or halving instead of taking the cube root.',
  'MAT-8-1|Taxicab Numbers':
    '**What it is:** A number that can be written as the sum of two positive cubes in two different ways. **Why it works:** The smallest is 1729, because 1 cubed plus 12 cubed is 1729 and 9 cubed plus 10 cubed is also 1729; one expression is never enough, since both sums must reach the same total from different pairs. **Common mistake:** Accepting two expressions when one is not a sum of two cubes, or allowing a zero cube to make the trick work.',
  'MAT-8-2|Laws of Exponents':
    '**What it is:** Short rules for combining powers of the same base without multiplying every factor out. **Why it works:** Multiplying adds the exponents, so 2^5 x 2^3 = 2^8 = 256 and a^m x a^n = a^(m + n); dividing subtracts them, and a power raised to a power multiplies them. **Common mistake:** Multiplying the exponents while multiplying the bases, so 2^5 x 2^3 becomes 4^8, or adding the exponents of 2^3 x 3^4, which have different bases.',
  'MAT-8-2|Powers of 10 and Scientific Notation':
    '**What it is:** A way of writing very large or very small numbers as a number between 1 and 10 multiplied by a power of ten. **Why it works:** Each shift of the decimal point one place to the left raises the power by one, so 45,000 = 4.5 x 10^4, 4,500,000 = 4.5 x 10^6 and 20,800 = 2.08 x 10^4. **Common mistake:** Leaving the front number as 45 or 20.8 instead of between 1 and 10, and then miscounting the power of ten to compensate.',
  'MAT-8-2|Zero and Negative Exponents':
    '**What it is:** Two rules that carry powers below one: anything to the power zero is 1, and a negative power means a reciprocal. **Why it works:** Dividing a power by itself gives 1 and subtracts the exponents, so a^0 = 1 for any non-zero a; a negative exponent flips the base, so 2^-3 = 1/2^3 = 1/8 and 10^-3 = 0.001. **Common mistake:** Answering 7^0 as 0 or 7, or treating 2^-3 as -8 by flipping the answer\'s sign instead of the fraction.',
  'MAT-8-3|Base-n Number Systems':
    '**What it is:** A system where each place is worth n times the place to its right, so the landmarks are powers of n. **Why it works:** In base 2 the numeral 101 means 1 x 4 + 0 x 2 + 1 = 5; in base 5 the numeral 23 means 2 x 5 + 3 = 13. Two landmarks multiplied give another landmark, which makes multiplication easy. **Common mistake:** Reading a base-5 or base-2 numeral as though it were decimal, so 23 in base 5 is taken as twenty-three.',
  'MAT-8-3|Place Value Representation and the Abacus':
    '**What it is:** The value a digit carries because of its column, shown on a spike abacus by beads. **Why it works:** Every column is ten times the one to its right, so in 4,305 the 3 stands for 3 hundreds, that is 300, and moving one place left makes a digit worth ten times more; each abacus spike is one place value. **Common mistake:** Giving the place value as the digit itself, writing 3 instead of 300, or moving the wrong way and dividing.',
  'MAT-8-3|Roman Numerals':
    '**What it is:** A system built from seven symbols - I, V, X, L, C, D and M - used by adding and subtracting. **Why it works:** A smaller symbol before a larger one is subtracted and after it is added, so 42 is XLII, meaning 40 + 2, and 90 is XC, meaning 100 - 10; only I, X and C may be subtracted, and only from the next two values. **Common mistake:** Writing 90 as LXXXX or 42 as XXXXII, when four repeats should be replaced by a subtraction.',
  'MAT-8-4|Angles of a Quadrilateral':
    '**What it is:** The four interior angles of any quadrilateral always add up to 360 degrees, however unevenly the shape is drawn. **Why it works:** A single diagonal cuts the quadrilateral into two triangles, each worth 180 degrees, so three angles of 80, 100 and 120 degrees leave 360 - 300 = 60 degrees for the fourth. **Common mistake:** Using 180 degrees as the total, or adding the three given angles and offering that sum as the missing angle.',
  'MAT-8-4|Kite and Trapezium':
    '**What it is:** A trapezium has one pair of parallel sides; a kite has two pairs of adjacent equal sides. **Why it works:** Between parallel sides, angles on the same side of a crossing line add to 180 degrees, so with A 65 and B 75, D is 115 and C is 105; a kite\'s equal pairs meet along its line of symmetry. **Common mistake:** Giving a trapezium two pairs of parallel sides, or pairing opposite sides in a kite when the equal sides are adjacent.',
  'MAT-8-4|Parallelogram and Rhombus':
    '**What it is:** A parallelogram has both pairs of opposite sides parallel and equal, a rhombus adds all four sides equal. **Why it works:** Opposite angles are equal and adjacent angles add to 180 degrees, so (2x + 10) + 3x = 180 gives x = 34; a rhombus also has diagonals that bisect each other at right angles. **Common mistake:** Assuming every parallelogram is a rhombus, or that its diagonals are always equal.',
  'MAT-8-5|Digits in Disguise':
    '**What it is:** Reading a numeral as a sum of place values, so a letter standing for a digit can be handled as a number. **Why it works:** The tens digit is worth ten times its face value, so 3a means 30 + a and 4z2 means 400 + 10z + 2; a digit-sum test then finds the unknown, since 6 + z must be a multiple of 9, making z equal 3. **Common mistake:** Treating 3a as 3 multiplied by a, when the a is a single digit in the units place.',
  'MAT-8-5|Divisibility by 3, 9 and 11':
    '**What it is:** Quick digit tests that show whether a number divides exactly, without doing the division. **Why it works:** A number is divisible by 3 or 9 when its digit sum is, so 4,356 gives 4 + 3 + 5 + 6 = 18, divisible by both; for 11, add alternate digits, subtract the other group, and the result must be 0 or a multiple of 11. **Common mistake:** Using the plain digit sum for 11, which needs alternating signs, or rejecting a remainder of 0.',
  'MAT-8-5|Remainders and Divisibility':
    '**What it is:** Divisibility rules read the last digit or the digit sum, while a remainder says what is left after the largest multiple. **Why it works:** A number divisible by 2 ends in an even digit and one divisible by 5 ends in 0 or 5; every number leaving remainder 3 on division by 5 has the form 5n + 3, so 3, 8, 13 and 18 fit with n = 0, 1, 2 and 3. **Common mistake:** Forgetting that n may be 0, so the smallest such number is missed.',
  'MAT-8-6|Algebraic Identities':
    '**What it is:** Standard expansions that are true for every value of the letters, so a product of brackets can be written without multiplying term by term. **Why it works:** (a + b)^2 = a^2 + 2ab + b^2, (a - b)^2 = a^2 - 2ab + b^2, and (a + b)(a - b) = a^2 - b^2; so (x + 5)^2 = x^2 + 10x + 25 while (x + 5)(x - 5) = x^2 - 25. **Common mistake:** Writing (a + b)^2 as a^2 + b^2 and dropping the middle 2ab term, which is where most of the value sits.',
  'MAT-8-6|How a Product Increases or Decreases':
    '**What it is:** How the answer to a multiplication changes when one or both of the factors are changed. **Why it works:** Doubling one factor doubles the product, and doubling both makes it four times as large; the same reasoning lets you split awkward products, so 96 x 104 = (100 - 4)(100 + 4) = 10,000 - 16 = 9,984. **Common mistake:** Assuming two changes cancel out, or doubling one factor and expecting the product to become four times larger.',
  'MAT-8-6|This Way or That Way, All Ways Lead to the Bay':
    '**What it is:** Different routes to a product - splitting, regrouping, using an identity - all arrive at the same answer. **Why it works:** The distributive property lets you break 6 x 14 into 6 x 10 + 6 x 4 = 84, and the difference of squares handles 32 x 28 as (30 + 2)(30 - 2) = 900 - 4 = 896, so a hard product becomes easy. **Common mistake:** Trusting one method blindly, so a slip in that route is accepted instead of checked another way.',
  'MAT-8-7|Problem Solving with Proportional Reasoning':
    '**What it is:** Using a steady rate or fixed ratio to work out an unknown amount from a known one. **Why it works:** Find one unit first, then multiply: 180 km in 4 hours is 45 km an hour, so 405 km takes 405 / 45 = 9 hours, and a recipe needing 200 g for 4 people gives 50 g each, so 10 people need 500 g. **Common mistake:** Setting the ratio the wrong way round, so the answer grows when it should shrink.',
  'MAT-8-7|Ratios in their Simplest Form':
    '**What it is:** A ratio compares two quantities of the same kind by division, and simplest form has no common factor left. **Why it works:** Divide both parts by their highest common factor: 45 : 60 divides by 15, 18 : 24 by 6 and 72 : 96 by 24, and each gives 3 : 4. **Common mistake:** Dividing only one side of the ratio, or subtracting the same number from both parts instead of dividing.',
  'MAT-8-7|Sharing, but Not Equally, and Unit Conversions':
    '**What it is:** Dividing a whole in a ratio, and converting units so the parts can be compared. **Why it works:** Add the ratio terms to count the parts, then divide: 4,200 rupees in the ratio 3 : 4 gives 7 parts of 600 rupees, so Ravi gets 1,800 and Meena 2,400; 3.5 kg is 3,500 g and 2.5 km is 2,500 m, since each step is a thousand. **Common mistake:** Handing out the ratio numbers as fractions without dividing by the sum of the terms.',
  'MAT-8P2-1|Percentage Increase, Decrease, Profit and Loss':
    '**What it is:** Expressing a change as a fraction of the original amount and multiplying by 100. **Why it works:** The original figure is the base, so a chair bought at 1,200 rupees and sold at 1,500 gains 300, and 300 / 1200 x 100 = 25 per cent; an item priced at 500 and sold at 450 loses 50, and 50 / 500 x 100 = 10 per cent. **Common mistake:** Dividing the change by the selling price instead of the cost price.',
  'MAT-8P2-1|Percentage of a Quantity':
    '**What it is:** Finding a stated fraction of an amount - what 25 per cent of 240 comes to. **Why it works:** A per cent is a fraction out of a hundred, so 25 per cent of 240 is 25/100 x 240 = 60, and 12 per cent of 750 students is 0.12 x 750 = 90 students, the same as finding one per cent and multiplying. **Common mistake:** Adding or subtracting the percentage instead of multiplying by it, or shifting the decimal point the wrong way.',
  'MAT-8P2-1|The FDP Trio: Fractions, Decimals and Percentages':
    '**What it is:** Three ways of writing the same part of a whole, linked by factors of 100. **Why it works:** Since 0.125 x 100 = 12.5, the decimal 0.125 is 12.5 per cent and also the fraction 1/8; 0.25 is 1/4, and 40 per cent is 0.4 or 2/5. Decimal to per cent multiplies by 100, per cent to decimal divides by 100. **Common mistake:** Writing 0.125 as 1.25 per cent, or 40 per cent as 0.04, by moving the point one place instead of two.',
  'MAT-8P2-2|Baudhayana’s Theorem on Right-angled Triangles':
    '**What it is:** The rule that in a right triangle the square on the hypotenuse equals the sum of the squares on the other two sides. **Why it works:** Legs 6 cm and 8 cm give a hypotenuse of root(36 + 64) = 10 cm, and a 13 m ladder with its foot 5 m from the wall reaches root(169 - 25) = 12 m up; the hypotenuse is always opposite the right angle. **Common mistake:** Adding the hypotenuse\'s square to a leg\'s, or treating the longest side as a leg.',
  'MAT-8P2-2|Halving a Square':
    '**What it is:** The size of the square you get when the area is halved, or when midpoints of a square are joined. **Why it works:** Area is side squared, so halving the area divides the side by root 2: a 6 cm square becomes 3 root 2 cm, and a 10 cm square becomes about 7.07 cm, between 7 and 8; joining midpoints gives exactly half the area. **Common mistake:** Halving the side as well as the area, which quarters the area instead of halving it.',
  'MAT-8P2-2|Hypotenuse of an Isosceles Right Triangle':
    '**What it is:** The longest side of a right triangle whose two shorter sides are equal, always one equal side times root 2. **Why it works:** Equal sides of 3 cm give a hypotenuse of root(9 + 9) = 3 root 2 cm, and the square on the hypotenuse is double the square on an equal side, so a 12 cm hypotenuse gives 144 / 2 = 72 square cm. **Common mistake:** Forgetting to halve, so the hypotenuse\'s square is given as the area of the square on a leg.',
  'MAT-8P2-3|Dividing a Whole in a Given Ratio':
    '**What it is:** Splitting an amount into parts whose sizes follow the given numbers, rather than into equal shares. **Why it works:** Count the parts, find one part, then multiply: 63 in the ratio 4 : 5 gives 9 parts of 7, so 28 and 35, and 2,700 rupees in 2 : 3 : 4 gives 9 parts of 300, so C receives 1,200 rupees. **Common mistake:** Halving the total between two numbers, or treating 2 : 3 : 4 as fractions without adding the terms.',
  'MAT-8P2-3|Inverse Proportions':
    '**What it is:** A link between two quantities in which one grows while the other shrinks by the same factor. **Why it works:** The product stays constant, so 6 workers taking 12 days means the wall needs 6 x 12 = 72 worker-days, and 9 workers need 72 / 9 = 8 days; doubling the workers halves the time, and half the workers double it. **Common mistake:** Solving it like direct proportion, so more workers are given more days instead of fewer.',
  'MAT-8P2-3|Ratios with More than Two Terms':
    '**What it is:** A ratio that shares one whole among three or more groups in a single expression, such as 2 : 3 : 5. **Why it works:** Add every term to count the parts: 2 + 3 + 5 = 10, so with a total of 100 one part is 10 and c is 50; angles in the ratio 1 : 3 : 5 make 9 parts of 20 degrees, giving 20, 60 and 100 degrees. **Common mistake:** Adding only two of the terms, or dividing by the largest term instead of the sum of all of them.',
  'MAT-8P2-4|Koch Snowflake and Fractals in Art':
    '**What it is:** A shape built by repeating one change at a smaller scale, such as the Koch snowflake. **Why it works:** Each segment becomes 4 segments a third as long, so the perimeter is multiplied by 4/3 each step and grows without limit, while the area stays finite - which is what makes it a fractal. **Common mistake:** Assuming a shape in a small area must have a short boundary, or one step as the finished fractal.',
  'MAT-8P2-4|Nets, Unfolding and the Shortest Path':
    '**What it is:** A net is a flat shape that folds into a solid, showing every face at once. **Why it works:** The net of a cube is 6 squares, so a cube of edge 4 cm needs 6 x 16 = 96 square cm of paper; unfolding also turns a route across the surface into a straight line, which is the shortest path. **Common mistake:** Counting only the faces you can see, or assuming the shortest path over a box runs along its edges.',
  'MAT-8P2-4|Projections: Front, Top and Side Views':
    '**What it is:** Flat drawings of a solid as seen from three directions - front, top and side. **Why it works:** Each view flattens one dimension, so a cuboid measuring 5 cm by 3 cm by 2 cm shows a 5 x 3 rectangle from above, a 5 x 2 from the front and a 3 x 2 from the side; three views together pin down the shape. **Common mistake:** Drawing the front view in the place of the top view, or giving the same rectangle for every view.',
  'MAT-8P2-5|How Changing Values Moves the Mean':
    '**What it is:** The mean is the total divided by the count, so any value added or removed pulls it towards itself. **Why it works:** Adding a very large value raises the total faster than the count, so the mean goes up; with 8 values averaging 25 the total is 200, and the remaining 7 averaging 24 total 168, so the removed value is 32. **Common mistake:** Averaging the two means together, instead of working back to the totals.',
  'MAT-8P2-5|Infographics and Interpretation':
    '**What it is:** A picture or chart presenting information so it can be understood at a glance. **Why it works:** Each shape stands for a number, so 25 per cent of a 32,000 rupee income is 8,000 rupees of rent; but a stretched picture or a broken scale can make a small difference look huge, so read the labels and the scale before trusting the size. **Common mistake:** Comparing the drawn sizes of the icons instead of the numbers they represent.',
  'MAT-8P2-5|Line Graphs and Trends':
    '**What it is:** A graph that joins plotted points to show how a quantity changes over time. **Why it works:** The slope carries the meaning - a rising line is an increasing trend, a falling line a decrease, and a flat horizontal segment means the value stayed the same between those readings, however high on the graph. **Common mistake:** Reading only the height of the line and ignoring its direction, so a flat segment is reported as zero.',
  'MAT-8P2-6|Calendar Magic and Algebra Grids':
    '**What it is:** Using the fixed layout of a calendar, seven days to a row, to find unknown dates with algebra. **Why it works:** The date directly below another is that date plus 7, so the four numbers of a 2 x 2 block are n, n + 1, n + 7 and n + 8, which add to 4n + 16; setting 4n + 16 = 64 gives n = 12, the smallest of the four. **Common mistake:** Adding 1 instead of 7 when moving down a row, which breaks every later step of the equation.',
  'MAT-8P2-6|Number Pyramids':
    '**What it is:** A triangle of bricks in which each brick holds the sum of the two bricks directly beneath it. **Why it works:** Adding happens once per level, so a bottom row a, b, c gives a + b and b + c above, then a + 2b + c at the top; the middle brick counts twice, and with 3 and 5 the brick above is 8. **Common mistake:** Filling the top brick with the sum of the whole bottom row, or forgetting that the middle brick is counted twice.',
  'MAT-8P2-6|The Largest Product and Decoding Divisibility Tricks':
    '**What it is:** Two tricks: the biggest product from a fixed sum, and why reversing digits gives a multiple of 9. **Why it works:** For a fixed sum the product is largest when the numbers are close, so 10 x 10 = 100 beats 19 x 1; and 74 - 47 = 27 = 9 x 3, because swapping digits changes the value by 9 times their difference. **Common mistake:** Believing the most lopsided pair gives the largest product, or that reversing digits gives no pattern.',
  'MAT-8P2-7|Area of a Parallelogram':
    '**What it is:** The space inside a four-sided shape with parallel opposite sides, in square units. **Why it works:** Cutting a triangle from one end and moving it to the other turns the parallelogram into a rectangle with the same base and height, so the area is base x height: a base of 14 cm and a height of 9 cm give 126 square cm. **Common mistake:** Multiplying the sloping side by the height instead of the perpendicular height.',
  'MAT-8P2-7|Area of a Rhombus and a Trapezium':
    '**What it is:** Two area formulas: the rhombus and the trapezium. **Why it works:** A rhombus is half the rectangle drawn around its diagonals, so its area is 1/2 x d1 x d2: diagonals of 16 cm and 12 cm give 96 square cm, and a side is root(8 squared + 6 squared) = 10 cm. A trapezium is half the sum of its parallel sides times the height. **Common mistake:** Using base x height with a slant side as the base, or dropping the half.',
  'MAT-8P2-7|Area of a Triangle':
    '**What it is:** The space enclosed by three sides, which is always exactly half the matching parallelogram. **Why it works:** A triangle standing on the same base and the same height as a parallelogram covers half of it, so the area is 1/2 x base x height - a base of 10 cm and a height of 6 cm give 30 square cm. **Common mistake:** Using a slant side as the height, or forgetting the factor of one half and doubling the correct answer.',
  'SCI-8-1|Careful Observation':
    '**What it is:** Noticing the details of an experiment patiently and exactly, using your senses and simple tools. **Why it works:** Careful observation gives evidence instead of guesswork, so every reading is written with its unit - a bare 5 could be 5 cm or 5 m - and a hand lens or microscope extends the eye to details too small to see. **Common mistake:** Recording a guess or a memory as an observation, or writing the number without its unit.',
  'SCI-8-1|Controlled Variables in an Experiment':
    '**What it is:** The conditions you deliberately keep the same in every trial so that a comparison is fair. **Why it works:** If two things change at once you cannot tell which caused the difference you saw, so you change one variable and hold the rest constant; only then does the result point to a single cause. **Common mistake:** Thinking a test with nothing changed is fair - a fair test changes exactly one thing and fixes everything else.',
  'SCI-8-1|Recording Data and Making Sense of It':
    '**What it is:** Writing down what you changed, saw and measured while it is still fresh. **Why it works:** Memory quietly rewrites details within hours, so notes taken at once are the only honest record, and a table places readings side by side so a pattern, such as a steady rise, becomes visible. **Common mistake:** Waiting until the experiment ends to write the results, or listing numbers without saying what was changed to produce them.',
  'SCI-8-10|Converging and Diverging Light':
    '**What it is:** Whether a mirror or lens brings parallel rays together at a point or spreads them apart. **Why it works:** A convex lens and a concave mirror converge light, so they can burn a hole in paper at the focus; a concave lens and a convex mirror diverge, so their rays never meet. **Common mistake:** Assuming a concave lens converges because a concave mirror does, when that lens spreads light out.',
  'SCI-8-10|Convex and Concave Lenses':
    '**What it is:** Two curved lenses told apart by which part is thicker. **Why it works:** A convex lens is thicker in the middle, so it converges light, magnifies, and corrects long-sightedness; a concave lens is thicker at its edges, diverges light, and corrects short-sightedness by spreading the rays before they reach the eye. **Common mistake:** Judging only by the curve of the surface, or swapping which defect each lens is meant to correct.',
  'SCI-8-10|Laws of Reflection':
    '**What it is:** The two rules a ray of light obeys when it reflects. **Why it works:** The angle of incidence equals the angle of reflection, and the incident ray, the reflected ray and the normal all lie in the same plane; a ray striking a plane mirror at 30 degrees to the normal therefore leaves at 30 degrees. **Common mistake:** Measuring the angle from the mirror\'s surface instead of the normal, which turns a 30 degree angle into 60.',
  'SCI-8-11|Lunar, Solar and Luni-solar Calendars':
    '**What it is:** Three ways of counting months and years: by the phases of the Moon, by the Sun, or by both together. **Why it works:** Twelve lunar months last about 354 days against a solar year of 365, so a luni-solar calendar adds an intercalary month, the Adhika Maasa, every two or three years to keep festivals in step with the seasons. **Common mistake:** Assuming a lunar year matches the solar year, so no correction is ever needed.',
  'SCI-8-11|The Solar Day, the Month and the Year':
    '**What it is:** The three natural cycles behind our units of time: the Earth turning, the Moon circling the Earth, the Earth circling the Sun. **Why it works:** A solar day runs from the Sun\'s highest point one day to the next, about 24 hours, and comes from the Earth\'s rotation; a year is one orbit of the Sun, about 365 days. **Common mistake:** Confusing rotation with revolution, so a day is described as the Earth going round the Sun.',
  'SCI-8-11|Waxing and Waning Periods':
    '**What it is:** The two halves of the lunar month, when the lit part of the Moon grows and then shrinks. **Why it works:** The Moon shines by reflected sunlight, so as it moves the visible lit portion changes: waxing grows towards the full Moon, waning shrinks after it, and on new moon night the lit half faces away, so no Moon is seen. **Common mistake:** Reversing the words and calling the growing phase waning, or expecting a Moon at new moon.',
  'SCI-8-12|Food Chains, Decomposition and Balance':
    '**What it is:** The path food takes from plants through animals and back to the soil, kept moving by decomposers. **Why it works:** Green plants are producers that trap sunlight, animals eat them, and decomposers break down dead matter and return nutrients to the soil so the cycle restarts; without them waste would pile up and the soil would starve. **Common mistake:** Leaving decomposers out of the chain, or thinking energy returns to the Sun.',
  'SCI-8-12|Interactions Among Organisms':
    '**What it is:** The ways two species living together affect each other - mutualism, commensalism and parasitism. **Why it works:** In mutualism both gain, in parasitism one gains while the host is harmed, and in commensalism one gains while the other is unaffected - an orchid on a tree branch gets support and takes nothing. **Common mistake:** Calling every partnership mutualism, so a parasite that harms its host gets described as helpful.',
  'SCI-8-12|Population, Community and Ecosystem':
    '**What it is:** Three levels of life: one species\' group, all species together, and all of that with the non-living surroundings. **Why it works:** All the deer in a forest form a population, all its populations form a community, and adding soil, water and sunlight makes an ecosystem, so each level contains the one below. **Common mistake:** Using ecosystem for animals only, and forgetting that the non-living part is half of it.',
  'SCI-8-13|Atmosphere, Ozone Layer and Magnetic Field':
    '**What it is:** Three protective shells around the Earth that make life possible. **Why it works:** The atmosphere supplies gases and warmth, the ozone layer absorbs harmful ultraviolet rays from the Sun, and the magnetic field deflects charged particles from the Sun before they strip the air away. **Common mistake:** Crediting the ozone layer with trapping heat or the magnetic field with blocking sunlight, when each stops a different threat.',
  'SCI-8-13|Reproduction and Threats to Life on Earth':
    '**What it is:** Why living things reproduce, and the three linked dangers facing life on the planet. **Why it works:** Reproduction passes traits on and keeps a species from dying out; the triple planetary crisis is climate change, biodiversity loss and pollution, three problems that worsen one another. **Common mistake:** Naming only one of the three, or imagining a species could survive without reproducing.',
  'SCI-8-13|Resources That Sustain Life on Earth':
    '**What it is:** The natural supplies life depends on, those that renew and those that do not. **Why it works:** Sunlight, wind and water renew, so they can be used again; coal and oil took millions of years to form, so they run out. The greenhouse effect holds just enough heat, and without an atmosphere the Earth would lose heat and freeze. **Common mistake:** Treating sunlight as exhaustible, or assuming the greenhouse effect is only harmful.',
  'SCI-8-2|Cell Wall, Plastids and Vacuole':
    '**What it is:** Three structures a plant cell has beyond what every cell shares. **Why it works:** The cell wall is a rigid outer layer giving shape, the chloroplasts hold chlorophyll and make food by photosynthesis, and a large vacuole stores water and keeps the cell firm, which is why a dry plant wilts. **Common mistake:** Saying the cell wall controls what enters, when that is the cell membrane, or hunting for chloroplasts in an animal cell.',
  'SCI-8-2|Levels of Organisation from Cell to Organism':
    '**What it is:** The ladder of building blocks that runs from a single cell up to a whole living body. **Why it works:** Similar cells form a tissue, tissues form an organ, organs form an organ system and systems make the organism, so nothing skips a step: a muscle cell, muscle tissue, the heart, the circulatory system, a human being. **Common mistake:** Putting the organ before the tissue, or jumping straight from cell to organism.',
  'SCI-8-2|Microorganisms and the Work They Do':
    '**What it is:** Living things too small to see without a microscope, and the useful work many of them do. **Why it works:** Yeast feeds on sugar and releases carbon dioxide that makes bread rise, bacteria turn milk into curd, and other microbes decompose waste and fix nitrogen in the soil; most microbes are harmless and many essential. **Common mistake:** Treating every microorganism as a germ, so the ones that make curd and bread are overlooked.',
  'SCI-8-3|Communicable and Non-communicable Diseases':
    '**What it is:** The difference between diseases that pass from person to person and those that do not. **Why it works:** Communicable diseases such as dengue spread through air, water or a carrier, while non-communicable ones such as diabetes develop from genes, diet and habits; a sign is what others can see, a symptom is what the patient feels. **Common mistake:** Calling every fever communicable, or confusing a visible sign with a symptom.',
  'SCI-8-3|Healthy Lifestyle and a Clean Environment':
    '**What it is:** The daily habits and surroundings that keep a person well, not merely free of infection. **Why it works:** Regular exercise and a balanced diet build resistance, and a clean environment cuts the routes infections travel - waste in dustbins, clean water and washed hands stop germs reaching a new host. **Common mistake:** Believing health is only the absence of disease, so a fit person living in filth is still called healthy.',
  'SCI-8-3|Vaccines, Antibiotics and Antibiotic Resistance':
    '**What it is:** Two medical tools: one trains the body to resist disease, the other kills the bacteria causing it. **Why it works:** A vaccine shows the body a harmless form of the germ so defences are ready; antibiotics kill bacteria only, so finish all of it, because survivors of a partial dose multiply and become resistant. **Common mistake:** Taking antibiotics for a cold or flu, which are viral, or stopping once you feel better.',
  'SCI-8-4|Electromagnets and Lifting Electromagnets':
    '**What it is:** A magnet made by passing a current through a coil, which behaves as a magnet only while the current flows. **Why it works:** Increasing the turns of the coil or the current makes it stronger, and switching off the current drops the load, which is exactly why a scrap yard can lift a heap of iron and release it where it wants. **Common mistake:** Expecting it to keep its magnetism after the current stops, the way a bar magnet does.',
  'SCI-8-4|Heating Effect of Electric Current and Resistance':
    '**What it is:** The way a current makes a wire hot, and why some wires get hotter than others. **Why it works:** More resistance means more heat for the same current, so nichrome is used in irons and room heaters while copper, with low resistance, carries current without glowing. **Common mistake:** Thinking the thickest wire heats most, when a thicker wire has less resistance and produces less heat.',
  'SCI-8-4|Voltaic Cell, Dry Cell and Rechargeable Batteries':
    '**What it is:** Devices that turn chemical energy into electrical energy through two terminals. **Why it works:** A dry cell\'s zinc container is the negative terminal and its carbon rod the positive, with a thick moist paste as the electrolyte, which is why it is called dry; a rechargeable battery can be recharged and used again. **Common mistake:** Calling a cell dry because it holds no paste, or expecting it to be recharged.',
  'SCI-8-5|Contact and Non-contact Forces':
    '**What it is:** Forces that need two surfaces to touch, and forces that act across a gap. **Why it works:** Friction, tension and the push of a hand need contact, while gravity, magnetism and static electricity act at a distance - a magnet pulls a pin without touching it, and gravity pulls a falling ball down through the air. **Common mistake:** Calling magnetic force a contact force because the magnet is held close to the object it lifts.',
  'SCI-8-5|Upthrust, Floating and Sinking':
    '**What it is:** The upward push a liquid gives to anything in it, judged against the object\'s weight. **Why it works:** An object floats when the upthrust equals its weight and sinks when its weight is greater, which is why a large wooden block floats while a small steel coin sinks - size and weight alone do not decide it. **Common mistake:** Assuming a heavier or bigger object must sink, when only upthrust against weight matters.',
  'SCI-8-5|Weight versus Mass':
    '**What it is:** Two different measurements of an object: how much matter it contains, and how strongly gravity pulls on it. **Why it works:** Mass is measured in kilograms and does not change with place, while weight is a force measured in newtons and depends on gravity, so 1 kg weighs about 10 N on Earth but only about 1.6 N on the Moon. **Common mistake:** Using the two words interchangeably, or claiming the mass becomes smaller on the Moon.',
  'SCI-8-6|Atmospheric Pressure':
    '**What it is:** The force with which the air above presses on everything at the Earth\'s surface. **Why it works:** Air has weight, so pressure falls as you climb because less air lies above you; a rubber sucker sticks because the pressure outside is greater than inside, which makes it hard to pull off. **Common mistake:** Thinking the sucker is glued down or that the air inside pulls it, when the outside air does the pushing.',
  'SCI-8-6|Formation of Wind and High-speed Winds':
    '**What it is:** Moving air, set going by pressure differences and turning violent when they are large. **Why it works:** Air flows from high pressure to low pressure, so a bigger difference means faster wind; leaving doors and windows open during a storm lets air move through and reduces the pressure difference over the roof. **Common mistake:** Saying wind blows from low pressure to high, or that a sealed house protects its roof.',
  'SCI-8-6|Thunderstorms, Lightning and Cyclones':
    '**What it is:** Violent weather built from rising warm air, ending in electric discharge or a spinning low-pressure centre. **Why it works:** Lightning is a huge electric discharge between cloud and ground; a cyclone is a large rotating storm whose centre, the eye, has the lowest pressure and calm air, ringed by strong wind and rain. **Common mistake:** Expecting the eye to be the most dangerous part, when it is the calm centre.',
  'SCI-8-7|Interparticle Spaces and Attractions':
    '**What it is:** The gaps between particles and the pull between them, which explain how matter behaves. **Why it works:** Gas particles are far apart, so a gas compresses easily; sugar stirred into water slips into the gaps between water particles, so the level falls; and a solid keeps its shape because its particles sit close with strong attraction. **Common mistake:** Believing the sugar vanishes when it dissolves, rather than filling the gaps.',
  'SCI-8-7|Melting Point and Boiling Point':
    '**What it is:** Two fixed temperatures at which a substance changes state. **Why it works:** The melting point is where a solid turns liquid, the boiling point where a liquid turns to vapour throughout its volume, and both stay steady during the change, since the heat breaks particles apart instead of raising the temperature. **Common mistake:** Expecting the temperature to keep rising during melting, or confusing evaporation with boiling.',
  'SCI-8-7|Solid, Liquid and Gaseous States':
    '**What it is:** The three everyday states of matter, told apart by whether shape and volume stay fixed. **Why it works:** A solid has a definite shape and a definite volume, a liquid keeps its volume but takes the shape of its container, and a gas keeps neither, because the particles are increasingly free to move and spread out. **Common mistake:** Saying a liquid has no definite volume, when it is the shape alone that changes with the container.',
  'SCI-8-8|Compounds Formed in Fixed Ratios':
    '**What it is:** Two or more elements joined chemically in one fixed proportion by mass. **Why it works:** The elements lose their own properties, so the compound can only be broken down chemically - iron heated with sulfur gives a black mass no magnet attracts; water is always hydrogen and oxygen in the same ratio. **Common mistake:** Calling a mixture a compound because the ingredients were stirred together.',
  'SCI-8-8|Elements and Their Properties':
    '**What it is:** A substance of only one kind of atom, which cannot be split into simpler substances chemically. **Why it works:** Iron, sulfur and oxygen are elements because every atom in them is of one kind, and their properties decide what they can do - sulfur is yellow and brittle, iron grey and magnetic. **Common mistake:** Treating air or steel as an element, or assuming a single name means one.',
  'SCI-8-8|Pure Substances and Their Separation':
    '**What it is:** A material with nothing else mixed in, made of one kind of particle. **Why it works:** A pure substance cannot be separated into other kinds of matter by any physical process, so distilled water counts but tap water does not; evaporation, filtration and distillation separate mixtures only. **Common mistake:** Reading pure as clean or safe, so filtered water is called pure though it holds dissolved salts.',
  'SCI-8-9|Density and Floating or Sinking':
    '**What it is:** How much mass is packed into each unit of volume. **Why it works:** Density is mass divided by volume, in kilograms per cubic metre, so an object floats in water when its density is less than water\'s and sinks when it is more - which is why iron floats as a hollow ship but not as a solid nail. **Common mistake:** Comparing weight or size alone instead of mass per unit volume, so a big light object is expected to sink.',
  'SCI-8-9|Saturated, Unsaturated, Dilute and Concentrated':
    '**What it is:** Four words for how much solute a solution holds at a temperature. **Why it works:** A dilute solution has little solute in much solvent, a concentrated one has a lot; a saturated solution cannot dissolve more at that temperature, so extra salt settles, while an unsaturated one can still take more. **Common mistake:** Using concentrated and saturated as synonyms, though a concentrated solution may still dissolve more.',
  'SCI-8-9|Solubility and the Effect of Temperature':
    '**What it is:** The maximum amount of a solute that a given amount of solvent can dissolve. **Why it works:** For most solids, heating the water raises solubility, so baking soda that would not dissolve at 20 degrees goes into solution at 50; the extra heat helps the particles break away and spread among the solvent particles. **Common mistake:** Assuming solubility always rises with heat, when gases actually dissolve less as the water warms.',
};
